rstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scheiber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54fd3433-aa27-56c8-88d1-eb9028501209', 'word_count': 920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54fd3433-aa27-56c8-88d1-eb9028501209'}, {'abstract': 'We can act as if God, morality and free will exist, even when we are certain they don’t.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/02/how-to-live-a-lie/', 'snippet': 'We can act as if God, morality and free will exist, even when we are certain they don’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few things are more annoying than watching a movie with someone who repeatedly tells you, “That couldn’t happen.” After all, we engage with artistic fictions by suspending disbelief. For the sake of enjoying a movie like “Back to the Future,” I may accept that time travel is possible even though I do not believe it. There seems no harm in that, and it does some good to the extent that it entertains and edifies me.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/02/opinion/02stoneWeb/02stoneWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/02/opinion/02stoneWeb/02stoneWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 763}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/02/opinion/02stoneWeb/02stoneWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 806, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/02/opinion/02stoneWeb/02stoneWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1611, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty