 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/03/us/politics/first-campaign-christie-button/first-campaign-christie-button-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/03/us/politics/first-campaign-christie-button/first-campaign-christie-button-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ad Helped Chris Christie Win a New Jersey Race, but Then He Had to Apologize', 'kicker': 'First Campaigns', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apologies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-02T10:09:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbebe5ac-d825-57cd-a601-0ae465152dda', 'word_count': 1030, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbebe5ac-d825-57cd-a601-0ae465152dda'}, {'abstract': 'Kasparov makes his case against the Putin presidency and points to the West’s role in his rule.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/winter-is-coming-by-garry-kasparov.html', 'snippet': 'Kasparov makes his case against the Putin presidency and points to the West’s role in his rule.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Russians in positions of power tend to measure their country’s standing in the world against the United States, longing to be recognized as equally important and powerful and getting very angry when they’re not so treated. President Vladimir Putin has made victimhood at America’s hands a leitmotif of his reign, and many Russians have bought into his claim that Washington tirelessly seeks ways to weaken, impoverish and otherwise humiliate their country. Many critics of Putin, in Russia and in the West, similarly hold that Washington’s treatment of Russia after the collapse of the Soviet Union was disdainful and thus undermined embryonic moves toward democracy, contributing to the rise of Putinism.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Schmemann01/1108-BKS-Schmemann01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Schmemann01/1108-BKS-Schmemann01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Schmemann01/1108-BKS-Schmemann01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Schmemann01/1108-BKS-Schmemann01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1537, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Schmemann01/1108-BKS-Schmemann01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Schmemann01/1108-BKS-Schmemann01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Schmemann01/1108-BKS-Schmemann01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Winter Is Coming,’ by Garry Kasparov', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exile’s Dissent ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasparov, Garry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greengard, Michael (Mig) (1969- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Winter Is Coming: Why Vladimir Putin and the Enemies of the Free World Must Be Stopped (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-02T10:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Serge Schmemann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmemann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/72078636-8d9c-50b1-bf88-d12889017c5d', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72078636-8d9c-50b1-bf88-d12889017c5d'}, {'abstract': 'The ascent of the ambitious autocrat from K.G.B. operative to self-appointed defender of his nation. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/the-new-tsar-the-rise-and-reign-of-vladimir-putin-by-steven-lee-myers.html', 'snippet': 'The ascent of the ambitious autocrat from K.G.B. operative to self-appointed defender of his nation. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vladimir Putin has an origin story. It takes place in Dresden in the fall of 1989, in the dying days of East Germany, on the night that thousands of protesters stormed the city’s Stasi headquarters. Once they were done ransacking the offices that had inspired so much terror, they directed their anger down the street toward the K.G.B. residence where Lieutenant Colonel Putin, a young intelligence officer, stood looking out the window. Watching the approaching mob, Putin called the local Soviet military command and asked for reinforcements. But no higher authority would approve it. “Moscow is silent,” he was told.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Beckerman01/1108-BKS-Beckerman01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Beckerman01/1108-BKS-Beckerman01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Beckerman01/1108-BKS-Beckerman01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 461, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Beckerman01/1108-BKS-Beckerman01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Beckerman01/1108-BKS-Beckerman01-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Beckerman01/1108-BKS-Beckerman01-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Beckerman01/1108-BKS-Beckerman01-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The New Tsar: The Rise and Reign of Vladimir Putin,’ by Steven Lee Myers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vladimir Rex', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Myers, Steven Lee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The New Tsar: The Rise and Reign of Vladimir Putin (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-02T10:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gal Beckerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gal', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beckerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9758a18d-5204-5183-a900-c291a53761ce', 'word_count': 1208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9758a18d-5204-5183-a900-c291a53761ce'}, {'abstract': 'Karl Ove Knausgaard reviews Michel Houellebecq’s new novel, which imagines France as a Muslim state in 2022.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/michel-houellebecqs-submission.html', 'snippet': 'Karl Ove Knausgaard reviews Michel Houellebecq’s new novel, which imagines France as a Muslim state in 2022.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before I begin this review, I have to make a small confession. I have never read Michel Houellebecq’s books. This is odd, I concede, since Houellebecq is considered a great contemporary author, and one cannot be said to be keeping abreast of contemporary literature without reading his work. His books have been recommended to me ever since 1998, most often “The Elementary Particles,” by one friend in particular, who says the same thing every time I see him. You have to read “The Elementary Particles,” he tells me, it’s awesome, the best book I’ve ever read. Several times I’ve been on the verge of heeding his advice, plucking “The Elementary Particles” from its place on my shelf and considering it for a while, though always returning it unread. The resistance to starting a book by Houellebecq is too great. I’m not entirely sure where it comes from, though I do have a suspicion, because the same thing goes for the films of Lars von Trier: When “Antichrist” came out I couldn’t bring myself to see it, neither in the cinema nor at home on the DVD I eventually bought, which remains in its box unwatched. They’re simply too good. What prevents me from reading Houellebecq and watching von Trier is a kind of envy — not that I begrudge them success, but by reading the books and watching the films I would be reminded of how excellent a work of art can be, and of how far beneath that level my own work is. Such a reminder, which can be crushing, is something I shield myself from by ignoring Houellebecq’s books and von Trier’s films. That may sound strange, and yet it can hardly be unusual. If you’re a carpenter, for instance, and you keep hearing about the amazing work of another carpenter, you’re not necessarily going to seek it out, because what would be the good of having it confirmed that there is a level of excellence to which you may never aspire? Better to close your eyes and carry on with your own work, pretending the master carpenter doesn’t exist.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 485, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 485}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 827, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1655, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD/1108-BKS-Knausgaard-LEAD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michel Houellebecq’s ‘Submission’', 'kicker': No