bsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdfbc0ec-842a-5c89-96f8-9e9497b36144', 'word_count': 646, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdfbc0ec-842a-5c89-96f8-9e9497b36144'}, {'abstract': 'One Brooklyn-born artist pays homage to her beloved team with embroidered works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/02/t-magazine/mets-baseball-art-elissa-goldstone.html', 'snippet': 'One Brooklyn-born artist pays homage to her beloved team with embroidered works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Driven out of the park, rebounded off a kneecap or ricocheted from a shattered bat into the stands — there are lots of ways to lose a ball, as evidenced by the last game of the 111th World Series on Sunday. On average, the series requires about 500 baseballs, all assembled by hand in much the same way they were a century ago: Each cork and rubber center is wrapped in yarn, encased in white cowhide and stitched together with waxed red thread. The same details inspire the Brooklyn-born and Mets-raised artist Elissa Goldstone’s work, which pays tribute to the days of knee-highs and chewing tobacco with highlight-reel scenes embroidered in bright, erratic lines; cotton sculptures of the four bases; and paper drawings done in familiar red double-stitch. For Goldstone, appreciating the minutiae of the game is the best way to cope with the inevitable heartbreak of being a baseball fan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/02/t-magazine/02tmag-mets/02tmag-mets-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/02/t-magazine/02tmag-mets/02tmag-mets-articleLarg