ia for promoting anti-Russian propaganda. Ms. Sharina denied the charges, saying the banned books were “planted during the searches,” a claim that was repeated by staff members. “Books cannot constitute extremism, because in my opinion extremism is an action,” she said in the courtroom, the Kommersant newspaper reported. Ms. Sharina’s lawyers have appealed the decision, the Interfax news agency said, and Human Rights Watch called for her immediate release in a statement.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Librarian Is Accused of Allowing Access to Banned Ukrainian Books', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Europe; Russia: Librarian Is Accused of Allowing Access to Banned Ukrainian Books', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharina, Natalia G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Korchynsky, Dmytro (1964- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Rights Watch', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-03T01:03:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ivan Nechepurenko', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ivan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nechepurenko', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1130dde8-39f9-50de-8422-2b9800d99fab', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://art