pe': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Randall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Randall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/502be395-e13f-559f-9127-698c0fe3a4c2', 'word_count': 1601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/502be395-e13f-559f-9127-698c0fe3a4c2'}, {'abstract': 'When the radio host speaks for angry Republicans in Iowa — and beyond — the 2016 candidates have no choice but to listen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/magazine/steve-deace-and-the-power-of-conservative-radio.html', 'snippet': 'When the radio host speaks for angry Republicans in Iowa — and beyond — the 2016 candidates have no choice but to listen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talk of alpha and beta males is common in the suburban Des Moines studio of the nationally syndicated conservative talk-show host Steve Deace. Almost daily, he assails the wimpy betas purporting to rule today’s Republican Party — the feckless politicians who, under siege, are giving ground to alpha fighters like him. Such chatter includes references to ‘‘manginas’’ and ‘‘growing a pair,’’ yet does not seem to put off the women in Deace’s audience; after all, being politically incorrect has become a conservative badge of honor in 2015.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '74', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08deace1/08deace1-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08deace1/08deace1-articleLarge-v6.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/