pe': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bb2c918-9c29-5cf4-8242-c7a7449f6970', 'word_count': 3564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bb2c918-9c29-5cf4-8242-c7a7449f6970'}, {'abstract': 'The memoirist believes in exposing the truth without talking about anyone’s penis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/magazine/mary-karr-thinks-you-shouldnt-google-yourself.html', 'snippet': 'The memoirist believes in exposing the truth without talking about anyone’s penis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In your book ‘‘The Art of Memoir,’’ you write that one of the side effects of being a memoirist is that strangers ‘‘skip the small talk and confide the most turbulent patches of their lives,’’ which to me wasn’t so surprising, but what you wrote about it was: ‘‘I have never not been moved by such a tale.’’ Small talk is boring. Politics are boring. The weather is boring. The minute somebody says to you, ‘‘I’ve never told anybody this before,’’ they’ve got your attention. I’m not a particularly nice person, but I’ve got to say, it makes me feel less lonely in the world when people confide in me.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '94', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08talk/08mag-08talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 932, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08talk/08mag-08talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 932}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08talk/08mag-08t