 Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-03T14:02:35+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew  Bate', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bate', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/109b88d1-5d1f-5cb0-965e-98314ab9d3ea', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/109b88d1-5d1f-5cb0-965e-98314ab9d3ea'}, {'abstract': 'This short documentary profiles the extreme sport of “UrbExing,” in which people infiltrate skyscrapers and subways for adventure — and the perfect photograph.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/03/opinion/vic-invades.html', 'snippet': 'This short documentary profiles the extreme sport of “UrbExing,” in which people infiltrate skyscrapers and subways for adventure — and the perfect photograph.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I first encountered the mysterious subject of this Op-Doc video, Victor Thomas, via his Instagram account, where he is known as “Vic Invades.” His photos showed someone dangling his feet over the edge of a massive skyscraper, limbs hanging precariously above the incredible vista of New York City far below. The images were striking – both beautiful and dangerous.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Vic Invades’', 'kicker': 'Op-Docs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-03T14:02:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Bate', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None,