rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Michigan Street Baptist Church (Buffalo, NY)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-03T20:29:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fffe3727-129e-51b5-b9ef-975aa180d6e1', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fffe3727-129e-51b5-b9ef-975aa180d6e1'}, {'abstract': 'Surreal, avant-garde works make up the new Art et Industrie retrospective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/03/t-magazine/art-et-industrie-retrospective-magen-h-gallery.html', 'snippet': 'Surreal, avant-garde works make up the new Art et Industrie retrospective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Art et Industrie, founded in 1977, was an influential avant-garde design movement beloved by the likes of Jean-Michel Basquiat, who frequented the group’s openings in SoHo. Years later, the gallerist Hugues Magen, newly arrived from Paris, was similarly struck: “I was educated in Europe, with all of the standards that this implies, and was confronted in New York with Art et Industrie,” recalls Magen, a former principal dancer with the Dance Theatre of Harlem. “I was mesmerized and challenged, in front of something for which I had very little reference, and I was inspired by seeing liberty in such a way that I was not accustomed to in terms of design.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Design Show Fit for Beetlejuice', 'kicker': 'Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 2, 