value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-04T11:55:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/308bd71f-5780-5132-8046-14fa65ed2ddf', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/308bd71f-5780-5132-8046-14fa65ed2ddf'}, {'abstract': 'Historians estimate that at least 1.2 million Koreans were coerced, or sometimes duped, into toiling as part of Japan’s war efforts in Japan, China and elsewhere.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/world/asia/south-korea-forced-war-laborers-japan-world-war-ii.html', 'snippet': 'Historians estimate that at least 1.2 million Koreans were coerced, or sometimes duped, into toiling as part of Japan’s war efforts in Japan, China and elsewhere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — Summing up a search that has been underway for more than a decade, a South Korean government commission said on Wednesday that the remains of nearly 2,750 people believed to be Koreans who were forced to work in Japan in the World War II era have been found.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remains of Nearly 2,750 Korean Wartime Laborers Found in Japan, South Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'World Briefing | Asia; South Korea: Wartime Remains Found', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koizumi, Junichiro', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-04T11:56:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae517c29-bb45-5571-989e-e63443b7168f', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae517c29-bb45-5571-989e-e63443b7168f'}, {'abstract': 'Keep your anatomical innuendos out of family game night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/magazine/judge-john-hodgman-on-rhymes-with-rock.html', 'snippet': 'Keep your anatomical innuendos out of family game night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lori writes: During a family game night, my husband drew a card that said, ‘‘Find two things that rhyme with ‘rock.’\u2009’’ He searched the house and returned with a sock and a tube of caulk. I immediately disputed the caulk, but he insisted it rhymes, shouting a mispronunciation of the word over and over in front of our 9-year-old.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08hodgman/08mag-08hodgman-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08hodgman/08mag-08hodgman-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 603}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08hodgman/08mag-08hodgman-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08hodgman/08mag-08hodgman-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1273, 'width': 1267, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08hodgman/08mag-08hodgman-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08hodgman/08mag-08hodgman-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08hodgman/08mag-08hodgman-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Judge John Hodgman on Rhymes With ‘Rock’', 'kicker': 'Judge John Hodgman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bonus Advice From Judge John Hodgman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hodgman, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-04T11:59:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Hodgman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodgman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02e803cf-bdaf-5320-9385-91899449919b', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02e803cf-bdaf-5320-9385-91899449919b'}, {'abstract': 'Is it fair to expend limited resources on one animal’s life when there is so much suffering in the world?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/magazine/how-much-can-we-spend-to-keep-our-dog-alive.html', 'snippet': 'Is it fair to expend limited resources on one animal’s life when there is so much suffering in the world?', 'lead_paragraph': 'About 18 months ago, my husband and I adopted a dog who was seized as part of a cruelty/neglect investigation. He has serious skin issues and was recently diagnosed with lymphoma. Like most dogs, despite all he has been through, he has a sweet doggy spirit. He also has a team of specialists — a dermatologist, an oncologist and his regular vet. At the moment, he is comfortable and happy. When the time comes (most likely in the next year), we will see him through to a gentle death. We have the resources to take care of him, but he is expensive — think car payment, not mortgage payment. He doesn’t look like a regular dog, and people know he has a medical routine. There is a lot of unsolicited judgment, mostly in three camps: those who think we should devote the same amount of resources to charitable animal-related organizations and not an individual dog, people who think it is wrong to ‘‘put a dog through chemo’’ and people who think it’s morally wrong to exert so much effort on an animal. We mostly just love our dog. What do you think? C. S., Portland, Ore.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/magazine/08ethicist/08mag-08ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 338, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {