None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/259a5c71-be56-5265-a753-ff32b8c3e05a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/259a5c71-be56-5265-a753-ff32b8c3e05a'}, {'abstract': 'By day, the VistaJet founder Thomas Flohr is attempting to revolutionize private aviation. But during his off hours, he gets to play secret agent at home in St. Moritz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/04/t-magazine/thomas-flohr-vistajet-home-st-moritz.html', 'snippet': 'By day, the VistaJet founder Thomas Flohr is attempting to revolutionize private aviation. But during his off hours, he gets to play secret agent at home in St. Moritz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'High above the Swiss ski resort of St. Moritz, where the royal and the merely rich break for caviar and champagne between downhill runs, is Thomas Flohr’s James Bond-inspired home, Ches’Aivla (Eagle’s Nest in the local language, Romansh). A seven-story, 35,000-square-foot structure built into the Suvretta mountainside, it has a cinema, library, wine cellar, spa, squash court, billiard room, bowling alley and pizzeria and is filled with important contemporary art and a stream of mostly female staff. Parked in the ‘‘garage gallery’’ is a pristine silver Aston Martin DB5 (‘‘like the one in ‘Goldfinger,’\u2009’’ Flohr says, brightly). Part of the home office is leather-padded like M’s at the MI6 headquarters in London, and there’s a midcentury rosewood and chrome desk that was used in ‘‘From Russia With Love’’ and ‘‘You Only Live Twice.’’ On the wall hangs a rusted steel map of the world with the capitals and major cities identified by tiny lights, ‘‘like when you have Dr. No in those meetings and they divide the world and look at the map,’’ he says.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '181', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/t-magazine/15tmag-12well_chalet-t_CA0/15tmag-12well_chalet-t_C