Natalie Digate Muth, M. D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sally', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sampson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': 'Digate', 'lastname': 'Muth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/062d1bc6-fcc3-52cb-bc30-9c07e81b5645', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/062d1bc6-fcc3-52cb-bc30-9c07e81b5645'}, {'abstract': 'Inside a converted delivery truck, Ian Ruhter manually creates stunning landscapes over the course of many hours.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/04/t-magazine/photographs-american-west-ian-ruhter-danziger.html', 'snippet': 'Inside a converted delivery truck, Ian Ruhter manually creates stunning landscapes over the course of many hours.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the photographer Ian Ruhter, whose first New York show opens tomorrow at Danziger Gallery, success has come from climbing into his work. “The game-changing moment came when I realized I could be the camera. I could be the mechanics of it,” Ruhter says. From inside the lens of a delivery truck converted into a camera, Ruhter becomes the machine’s very manual settings: the shutter and the processor, and also the “film,” which he makes from scratch on sheets of aluminum up to five feet wide. (Light-sensitive collodion plates, once wet, need to be exposed and developed in a matter of minutes, but the whole set-up takes about a day for a single image.) “It takes so much time,” he says, of choosing not to “jump out of a car and take a selfie” at a lookout. “It really makes me sit there and be part of the environment, and suck it up and enjoy.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When a Photographer Is His Own Camera', 'kicker': 'Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lake Tahoe (Nev)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yosemite National Park (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-04T19:15:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Su Wu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Su', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/792dd9c5-123b-59d2-9674-df103dc6ae6f', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/792dd9c5-123b-59d2-9674-df103dc6ae6f'}, {'abstract': 'As job options multiply, worker retention has risen to the top of the list of employers’ concerns. To many companies, “engagement” is the answer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/business/dealbook/one-downside-of-an-up-economy-employee-turnover.html', 'snippet': 'As job options multiply, worker retention has risen to the top of the list of employers’ concerns. To many companies, “engagement” is the answer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most employees — no matter how many years they have been with a company or how much talent and hard work they bring to the table — feel disposable. And most employers do little to discourage that feeling.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/business/dealbook/1105-DEALBOOK-WORK1/1105-DEALBOOK-WORK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/05/business/dealbook/1105-DEALBOOK-WORK1/11