multimedia', 'news_desk': 'NYT Now', 'section_name': 'NYT Now', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/d4102b58-c4ec-5c45-8d48-4c1b850dadf6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/d4102b58-c4ec-5c45-8d48-4c1b850dadf6'}, {'abstract': 'This couple’s show, “My Generation,” includes songs from Paul McCartney, Paul Simon and Billy Joel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/arts/music/review-john-pizzarelli-and-jessica-molaskey-feeling-deeply-at-cafe-carlyle.html', 'snippet': 'This couple’s show, “My Generation,” includes songs from Paul McCartney, Paul Simon and Billy Joel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was nearly 30 years ago that Paul Simon’s album “Graceland” introduced “The Boy in the Bubble,” a prescient song about our perilous, technologized world of “lasers in the jungle” and “the bomb in the baby carriage” with a fragmented expression of apocalyptic dread that still feels remarkably contemporary. And to hear Jessica Molaskey perform it on Tuesday evening with her husband, John Pizzarelli, on guitar at Café Carlyle was to be brought up short by the song’s telegraphic sense of alarm. Just like that bomb, the images explode in your mind.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: John Pizzarelli and Jessica Molaskey, Feeling Deeply, at Café Carlyle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hits From a Certain Era, Tenderly Played and Felt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cafe Carlyle (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pizzarelli, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Molaskey, Jessica', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-04T23:15:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e875a4e2-fec9-5c9f-bcdb-45032b73b221', 'word_count': 448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e875a4e2-fec9-5c9f-bcdb-45032b73b221'}, {'abstract': 'A plan involving mesothelioma patients’ being referred to a law firm, the heart of the corruption case against Mr. Silver, came into focus as Dr. Robert N. Taub of Columbia University testified.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/nyregion/doctor-tells-of-elaborate-arrangement-at-sheldon-silver-trial.html', 'snippet': 'A plan involving mesothelioma patients’ being referred to a law firm, the heart of the corruption case against Mr. Silver, came into focus as Dr. Robert N. Taub of Columbia University testified.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In New York’s ornate Capitol building in Albany, a plan two years in the making was taking root. Everyone would benefit: Victims of mesothelioma, a rare but deadly form of cancer caused by exposure to asbestos, would be sent to a reputable law firm; the firm would pick up new clients; and a well-regarded cancer research clinic would receive funds.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/nyregion/SILVER-y/SILVER-y-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/05/nyregion/SILVER-y/SILVER-y-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/nyregion/SILVER-y/SILVER-y-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width