jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Brokers', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-05T08:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/63320aea-cc83-5d98-a5fe-d82394227192', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63320aea-cc83-5d98-a5fe-d82394227192'}, {'abstract': 'He enabled Washington to sustain a fantasy future for Iraq.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/opinion/why-america-invented-ahmad-chalabi.html', 'snippet': 'He enabled Washington to sustain a fantasy future for Iraq.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WITH his death, the Iraqi politician Ahmad Chalabi is once again in the news. Detractors rage about his supply of fabricated intelligence on Iraqi weapons of mass destruction that supposedly tricked Washington into war. Supporters claim he was a heroic dissident who was never given the chance to transform his troubled country into paradise.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05roseWeb/05roseWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05roseWeb/05roseWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05roseWeb/05roseWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05roseWeb/05roseWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05roseWeb/05roseWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05roseWeb/05roseWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05roseWeb/05roseWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why America Invented Ahmad Chalabi', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why We Invented Chalabi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rose, Gideon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chalabi, Ahmad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-05T08:21:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gideon Rose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gideon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b084ad0-970b-54ea-8b72-26d374c804bc', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b084ad0-970b-54ea-8b72-26d374c804bc'}, {'abstract': 'The hit TV show, ending a 14-season run next year, transformed the way Americans view science and engineering. We’ll miss it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/opinion/what-we-owe-the-mythbusters.html', 'snippet': 'The hit TV show, ending a 14-season run next year, transformed the way Americans view science and engineering. We’ll miss it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN the reality TV show “MythBusters” debuted on the Discovery Channel in 2003, its producers weren’t on a mission to transform science and education in America. They just wanted to entertain. In each episode, the hosts would try to debunk or confirm a few classic urban legends. Could a penny dropped off the Empire State Building really kill a person? Could eating a poppy-seed bagel actually make you test positive for heroin? The producers cast two San Francisco-based special-effects artists, Jamie Hyneman and Adam Savage, as hosts. The show was a surprise hit — pulling in as many as 20 million viewers a season — and it helped changed our culture.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05meigs/05meigs-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05meigs/05meigs-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 500}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05meigs/05meigs-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/opinion/05meigs/05meigs-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1404, 'width': 1686, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name'