rp Dot Com', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-11-05T13:48:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b50bdf70-92c0-512f-9492-9fcb795e7c11', 'word_count': 71, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b50bdf70-92c0-512f-9492-9fcb795e7c11'}, {'abstract': 'The coming runoff in Argentina’s presidential election finds the nation in a mood for change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/06/opinion/a-post-peronist-president-at-last.html', 'snippet': 'The coming runoff in Argentina’s presidential election finds the nation in a mood for change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BUENOS AIRES — The last time Argentina was scheduled to hold a presidential runoff election was 12 years ago. That occasion was very unusual in that the winning candidate, intimidated by the thin margin of his victory, decided to quit the field. Rather than risk losing in the second round of voting, he chose to step down, leaving the presidency to the candidate who had been the runner-up in the first round.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/18/opinion/18uki-goni/18uki-goni-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/02/18/opinion/18uki-goni/18uki-goni-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/02/18/opinion/18uki-goni/18uki-goni-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1023, 's