': 'By Susanne Craig and Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f4ffa19-0915-584c-ae42-e73016652a39', 'word_count': 890, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f4ffa19-0915-584c-ae42-e73016652a39'}, {'abstract': 'Plus: one’s obligation to food-challenged co-workers; responding to the self-invited guest; and wearing a ring with a history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/fashion/bilingual-etiquette-for-in-laws.html', 'snippet': 'Plus: one’s obligation to food-challenged co-workers; responding to the self-invited guest; and wearing a ring with a history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our son lives across the country from us. We visit twice a year. My daughter-in-law, who was raised abroad but speaks perfect English, wants my granddaughter, 4, to be bilingual. She has only spoken to her in her native language from the day she was born. This doesn’t change when we visit. We sit clueless for long stretches while they talk. The same thing happens to my son, who barely speaks their language. We don’t object to our granddaughter learning another language, but we don’t want to feel so left out. Do you agree that this behavior is totally rude?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank'