'Thursday’s sale left the auction house feeling encouraged enough to announce that it was on track to cover the $500 million it had guaranteed in selling the collection of its former chairman, A. Alfred Taubman.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/sothebys-day-auction-of-taubman-collection-at-42-7-million/', 'snippet': 'Thursday’s sale left the auction house feeling encouraged enough to announce that it was on track to cover the $500 million it had guaranteed in selling the collection of its former chairman, A. Alfred Taubman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Daytime auctions don’t usually make big news. But at Sotheby’s Taubman Modern and Contemporary Art sale on Thursday afternoon, the results, which totaled $42.7 million with fees, left the auction house feeling encouraged enough to announce that it was on track to cover the $500 million it had guaranteed in selling the collection of its former chairman, A. Alfred Taubman. With 123 scheduled lots, the estimate for the sale was $30.2 million to $43.4 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sotheby’s Day Auction of Taubman Collection at $42.7 Million', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Records and Achievements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Thomas S Jr (Tad)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taubman, A Alfred', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Neue Galerie', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-05T21:49:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19096a8b-40a5-50f2-afc2-bb8a3520bfb7', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19096a8b-40a5-50f2-afc2-bb8a3520bfb7'}, {'abstract': 'For tonight’s event, the choreographer Tom Gold has reinterpreted a 1973 composition by Minsa Craig.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/t-magazine/guggenheim-gala-ballet-tom-gold-november-steps.html', 'snippet': 'For tonight’s event, the choreographer Tom Gold has reinterpreted a 1973 composition by Minsa Craig.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What do the painter Alberto Burri and the author Jacqueline Susann have in common? At first glance, not much. But leave it to the Guggenheim to mash up the incongruous for the sake of art — or in this case, for the Guggenheim International Gala, which for the last three years has been lent some extra oomph by Dior. Currently, the Guggenheim’s main floorspace is devoted to a retrospective of work by Burri, the Italian iconoclast whose postwar rejection of pictorial tradition influenced Arte Povera and Neo-Dada. The entertainment at tonight’s gala will come via Tom Gold, the former New York City Ballet principal now leading his own company, who has unearthed “November Steps,” an overlooked ballet by Burri’s wife Minsa Craig. “To me, it looked like Martha Graham and Merce Cunningham en pointe in Andy Warhol’s factory,” Gold says. “Or ‘Valley of the Dolls!’”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Very Mod Ballet at the Guggenheim Gala', 'kicker': 'Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guggenheim, Solomon R, Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-05T21:58:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Baumgardner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baumgardner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87abdba8-d392-5779-9a2b-c695a3013f47', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87abdba8-d392-5779-9a2b-c695a3013f47'}, {'abstract': 'Guralnick’s biography explores how Phillips passionately shaped a mix of black and white voices at Sun Records in Memphis, from Elvis Presley to Jerry Lee Lewis to Howlin’ Wolf.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/06/books/review-sam-phillips-the-man-who-invented-rock-n-roll-by-peter-guralnick.html', 'snippet': 'Guralnick’s biography explores how Phillips passionately shaped a mix of black and white voices at Sun Records in Memphis, from Elvis Presley to Jerry Lee Lewis to Howlin’ Wolf.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sam Phillips, the founder of Sun Records in Memphis, is frequently remembered for a single sound bite: “If I could find a white man who had the Negro sound and the Negro feel, I could make a billion dollars!” He seems cynical at best, racist at worst.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06BOOK/06BOOK-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06BOOK/06BOOK-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 771}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06BOOK/06BOOK-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 797, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 