hattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-05T22:57:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2da368e0-4c93-5dbd-81df-e1e7fc0c2f31', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2da368e0-4c93-5dbd-81df-e1e7fc0c2f31'}, {'abstract': 'The exhibition, drawn from the museum’s permanent collection, includes 30 or so artists, mostly European, who insisted on the primacy of the figure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/06/arts/design/soldier-spectre-shaman-an-alternate-history-at-moma.html', 'snippet': 'The exhibition, drawn from the museum’s permanent collection, includes 30 or so artists, mostly European, who insisted on the primacy of the figure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1936, the first director of the Museum of Modern Art mapped out the art of his time in a famous chart, the first taste of the museum’s persistent narrative of Modernism. The doctrine goes something like this: Western art of the early 20th century develops in a single direction, toward abstraction. Artists working toward that goal were the true radicals. Those who held onto representation were aesthetic conservatives (whatever their actual politics).Conveniently enough, the artists who broke most definitively with figuration, and who made abstract painting and sculpture into the One True Art, were living in New York — the city that, in the words of the art historian Serge Guilbaut, “stole the idea of Modern art” from Paris after World War II.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06FIG/06FIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 454, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06FIG/06FIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 454}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06FIG/06FIG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06FIG/06FIG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1548, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06FIG/06FIG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06FIG/06FIG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06FIG/06FIG-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Soldier, Spectre, Shaman,’ an Alternate History at MoMA', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Postwar Art That Defies Abstraction    ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farago, Jason', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giacometti, Alberto', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bacon, Francis', 'rank': 5, 'major':