 rainy-day fund instead. Critics say both ideas are wrongheaded.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The banking industry scored a surprise victory on Thursday when the House voted to pay for part of a new highway bill by draining a rainy-day fund at the Federal Reserve rather than cutting federal payments to some of the nation’s largest banks.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Congress Split on Tapping Fed or Banks to Fund Roads', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Congress Split on Tapping Fed or Banks to Fund Roads', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Highway Trust Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appelbaum, Binyamin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T00:20:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8be59381-6f4e-5a26-b82a-348783c79dde', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8be59381-6f4e-5a26-b82a-348783c79dde'}, {'abstract': 'Critics of recent and planned events say it is unseemly for members of the art world to support development efforts that would appear to result in gentrification.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/05/art-and-real-estate-collide-at-events-in-the-bronx-and-at-the-brooklyn-museum/', 'snippet': 'Critics of recent and planned events say it is unseemly for members of the art world to support development efforts that would appear to result in gentrification.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A major museum is scheduled to host a real estate conference addressing questions like “Where is the bleeding edge of neighborhood transformation?” and “What opportunities for value-add exist in the seemingly picked-over areas?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Art and Real Estate Collide at Events in the Bronx and at the Brooklyn Museum', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T00:21:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a488dc17-d71d-56ea-99ad-59721e89b9e6', 'word_count': 755, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a488dc17-d71d-56ea-99ad-59721e89b9e6'}, {'abstract': 'Written by Joseph Vasquez, the Bronx-born filmmaker who died in 1995, this kitchen sink drama overflows with street flavor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/06/movies/review-the-house-that-jack-built-a-gritty-busy-tale-of-the-city.html', 'snippet': 'Written by Joseph Vasquez, the Bronx-born filmmaker who died in 1995, this kitchen sink drama overflows with street flavor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Poor Jack (E. J. Bonilla) — so many things on his plate. His drug-dealing business at the corner bodega, which enabled him to buy the building where his family lives, is threatened by rival dealers; the marriage of his brother (Leo Minaya) is in trouble; he wants his sister (Rosal Colón), a lesbian, to stop seeing her girlfriend (and hopes to “cure” her by having a buddy sleep with her); his pregnant girlfriend (Melissa Fumero) won’t stop asking about marriage; and his alcoholic father (John Herrera) keeps bickering with his mother (Saundra Santiago). But we know that Jack is decent, because he beats up a drug addict pimping out his own daughter.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06HOUSEJACK/06HOUSEJACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06HOUSEJACK/06HOUSEJACK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06HOUSEJACK/06HOUSEJACK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/arts/06HOUSEJACK/06HOUSEJACK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJ