, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYS)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T01:16:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5dfb509-9850-5b71-ae8b-f3c7d4694886', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5dfb509-9850-5b71-ae8b-f3c7d4694886'}, {'abstract': 'The works on display at the Heckscher Museum of Art in Huntington are made by transferring an image from one medium to another, usually from a block, a plate or a screen to a sheet of paper.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/nyregion/graphic-appeal-explores-image-transfers-at-heckscher-museum-of-art.html', 'snippet': 'The works on display at the Heckscher Museum of Art in Huntington are made by transferring an image from one medium to another, usually from a block, a plate or a screen to a sheet of paper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some are serene landscapes. Others are vibrant abstracts. A few, like a whimsical depiction of flying slices of pizza, are mischievous illustrations. The works in “Graphic Appeal: Modern Prints From the Collection” at the Heckscher Museum of Art in Huntington cover a range of subjects and styles, but they have one thing in common: They were made by transferring an image from one medium to another, usually from a block, a plate or a screen to a sheet of paper.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08ARTSLI1/08ARTSLI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 304, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'imag