a': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On This Week Around the World', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dudamel, Gustavo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goya, Francisco de', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Gallery (London)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rockbund Art Museum', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Teatro Colon (Buenos Aires, Argentina)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guangzhou (China)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kolkata (India)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T15:13:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher D. Shea', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1c19fcf-7e62-5442-8647-f035be0f9f92', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1c19fcf-7e62-5442-8647-f035be0f9f92'}, {'abstract': 'The government edict may now be a thing of the past, but its impact will stretch far into the future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/06/magazine/the-long-shadow-of-chinas-one-child-policy.html', 'snippet': 'The government edict may now be a thing of the past, but its impact will stretch far into the future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you get stuck in a crowd in China — it’s not hard to do in a country of nearly 1.4 billion — you may hear someone mutter, “Ren tai duo!”: “Too many people!” It’s a common but misleading complaint. The real demographic crisis that prompted the Chinese government’s decision last week to end its one-child policy is more palpable on the quiet Shanghai lane where I live with my family: There is a dearth of young people.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/magazine/06mag-china-1/06mag-china-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/06/magazine/06mag-china-1/06mag-china-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/magazine/06mag-china-1/06mag-china-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/magazine/06mag-china-1/06mag-china-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1286, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/magazine/06mag-china-1/06mag-china-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/06/magazine/06mag-china-1/06mag-china-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/06/magazine/06mag-china-1/06mag-china-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Long Shadow of China’s One-Child Policy', 'kicker': 'Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deng Xiaoping', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xie Zuoshi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao Zedong', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Shanghai (China)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T15:29:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brook Larmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Larmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4a7b698-cece-5f88-9000-c3686f5bb84b', 'word_count': 1215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4a7b698-cece-5f88-9000-c3686f5bb84b'}, {'abstract': 'André Saraiva teamed up with the writers Glenn O’Brien and Olivier Zahm to reimagine the famous Hôtel Grand Amour.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/06/t-magazine/hotel-amour-paris.html', 'snippet': 'André Saraiva teamed up with the writers Glenn O’Brien and Olivier Zahm to reimagine the famous Hôtel Grand Amour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For nearly a decade, the tiny Hôtel Amour in Paris lured a certain clientele with its disco balls, black walls and phallic furniture. ‘‘There’s never room anymore,’’ says André Saraiva, the graffiti artist, nightlife impresario and co-owner. But with his new Hôtel Grand Amour, a Napoleon III-style building in the 10th Arrondissement with 44 rooms, some featuring libraries ‘‘curated’’ by the fashion writers Glenn O’Brien and Olivier Zahm, and art by the likes of Keith Haring and Petra Collins, he hopes to remedy that. There’s also a spa, a cafe, an upscale brasserie and a cocktail lounge inspired by the Ritz’s Bar Hemingway. ‘‘You could literally never leave,’’ says Saraiva.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '40', 's