: 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T17:54:48+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily S. Rueb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Rueb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/cd418745-89e4-516a-8f95-9f7d3f526519', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/cd418745-89e4-516a-8f95-9f7d3f526519'}, {'abstract': 'His family on the verge of homelessness, a boy summons an imaginary friend. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/crenshaw-by-katherine-applegate.html', 'snippet': 'His family on the verge of homelessness, a boy summons an imaginary friend. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 400-pound gorilla casts a large shadow. In 2013, Katherine Applegate won the Newbery Medal for “The One and Only Ivan,” the story of a gorilla kept in a strip-mall circus. Ivan, the gorilla, frees himself and a young elephant by communicating with humans through finger-painting. “Ivan” was a winning tale for many \xadreasons: It was based, loosely, on a true story; it addressed the issue of animal cruelty honestly, but in a manner that children could handle; it was technically original, telling the story from Ivan’s perspective, in short chapters that read like prose poems. But the primary reason “Ivan” has become such a beloved book is Ivan himself. His frank wisdom about life, both human and animal, provokes laughter and thought; his aspirations — to become an artist, and to liberate himself through art — feel universal. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Crenshaw,’ by Katherine Applegate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And One Enormous Cat', 'name': None, 'seo'