}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T17:59:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily S. Rueb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Rueb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/846c1435-35b7-5488-aa5b-7fe04f865f56', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/846c1435-35b7-5488-aa5b-7fe04f865f56'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Japan regards the re-election of President Roosevelt as ominous in 1940.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/06/1940-japanese-fear-poll-result/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Japan regards the re-election of President Roosevelt as ominous in 1940.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKIO — The re-election of President Roosevelt for a third term is regarded by Japanese in all circles as of ominous portent so far as American-Japanese relations are concerned. Japanese were hoping Wendell L. Willkie would be elected, not because they believed American pressure on Japan would be greatly relaxed, but because they considered a change might bring about a betterment, while they regarded a continuance of the Roosevelt policy as presaging further aggravation of relations with the United States. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/blogs/07iht-oldnov07-fdr/07iht-oldnov07-fdr-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/07/blogs/07iht-oldnov07-fdr/07iht-oldnov07-fdr-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 754}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/blogs/07iht-oldnov07-fdr/07iht-oldnov07-fdr-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 815, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/blogs/07iht-oldnov07-fdr/07iht-oldnov07-fdr-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1630, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/blogs/07iht-oldnov07-fdr/07iht-oldnov07-fdr-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/07/blogs/07iht-oldnov07-fdr/07iht-oldnov07-fdr-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/blogs/07iht-oldnov07-fdr/07iht-oldnov07-fdr-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1940: Japanese Fear Poll Result', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roosevelt, Franklin Delano', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T17:59:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8e4053a-f6aa-5647-ac63-9c02583cb947', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8e4053a-f6aa-5647-ac63-9c02583cb947'}, {'abstract': 'Blatter’s lawyer said the FIFA president was in a hospital for a ‘checkup’ but was expected to return home shortly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/07/sports/soccer/fifas-sepp-blatter-being-treated-for-stress-related-medical-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Blatter’s lawyer said the FIFA president was in a hospital for a ‘checkup’ but was expected to return home shortly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The suspended FIFA president Sepp Blatter was treated at a hospital on Friday but was expected to be released shortly, his lawyer said. An adviser to Mr. Blatter said the incident was “related to stress.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/sports/07BLATTER/07BLATTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/07/sports/07BLATTER/07BLATTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/sports/07BLATTER/07BLATTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/sports/07BLATTER/07BLATTER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/sports/07BLATTER/07BLATTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/07/sports/07BLATTER/07BLATTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/sports/07BLATTER/07BLATTER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'FIFA’s Sepp Blatter Being Treated for ‘Stress-Related’ Medical Issue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'FIFA President Is in Hospital ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blatter, Sepp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/911e93db-dd0d-5928-a774-d730418b3bfc', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/911e93db-dd0d-5928-a774-d730418b3bfc'}, {'abstract': 'When friends find a pool of toxic ooze, their town is quarantined and a cover-up exposed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/fuzzy-mud-by-louis-sachar.html', 'snippet': 'When friends find a pool of toxic ooze, their town is quarantined and a cover-up exposed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adults have it rough in children’s literature. Mothers vanish, fathers get slain, grim teen societies herd all the grown-ups into their own boring corners of the planet. It’s not that children’s authors dislike adults, exactly. It’s more an issue of plotting: Stories are more interesting when protagonists have the ability to change the world around them, for good or for bad — and kids will be the first to tell you that they become pretty powerless whenever adults are on the scene. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Fuzzy Mud,’ by Louis Sachar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mucking About', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sachar, Louis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Fuzzy Mud (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:03:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eliot Schrefer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eliot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schrefer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc4fcf50-42e0-51a4-ab04-109bf1868d1d', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc4fcf50-42e0-51a4-ab04-109bf1868d1d'}, {'abstract': 'A more tangible economic upswing could help Democrats keep the White House after the Obama administration’s long struggle with slow economic recovery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/07/business/economy/if-jobs-report-heralds-a-solid-upturn-democrats-fortunes-may-also-rise.html', 'snippet': 'A more tangible economic upswing could help Democrats keep the White House after the Obama administration’s long struggle with slow economic recovery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Friday’s surprisingly robust jobs report cheered Democrats hoping to retain the White House and capture the Senate next year, but it heralded a new atmosphere of uncertainty by flashing a green light to the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates, which could undermine economic growth.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/business/07employ-web1/07employ-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/07/business/07employ-web1/07employ-web1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/business/07employ-web1/07employ-web1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/business/07employ-web1/07employ-web1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/business/07employ-web1/07employ-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/07/business/07employ-web1/07employ-web1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/07/business/07employ-web1/07employ-web1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'If Jobs Report Heralds a Solid Upturn, Democrats’ Fortunes May Also Rise', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jobs Report Signals Shift in Economy, and Politics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appelbaum, Binyamin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Calmes, Jackie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:03:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes and Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62e14afa-d922-523e-86ef-c17e26ea5932', 'word_count': 1120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62e14afa-d922-523e-86ef-c17e26ea5932'}, {'abstract': 'The teenage protagonists of these young adult novels take up with disturbed older boys, with mixed results.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/the-trouble-in-me-and-orbiting-jupiter.html', 'snippet': 'The teenage protagonists of these young adult novels take up with disturbed older boys, with mixed results.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jack Gantos and his publisher encourage you to think of “The Trouble in Me” as a prequel to his celebrated memoir “Hole in My Life” — and I encourage you to ignore them. The books are so different in tone, in what they strive for and what they achieve, that they could meet at a party, get drunk and go home together without ever knowing that they’re cousins. Instead, let’s consider Gantos’s novel as spiritual kin to another new Y.A. novel from a highly decorated pro: Gary D. Schmidt’s “Orbiting Jupiter.” Here are two books about the perilous moment in a boy’s life when he is so desperate to define himself — so open to influence — that just getting to know a troubled older kid is like walking into a storm. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Trouble in Me’ and ‘Orbiting Jupiter’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Friendship’s Perils', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gantos, Jack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Gary D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Orbiting Jupiter (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Trouble in Me (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:04:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Giles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5391afe5-cc66-5af5-bbdb-d17ca663a574', 'word_count': 744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5391afe5-cc66-5af5-bbdb-d17ca663a574'}, {'abstract': 'The city’s famous air pollution is coming back. So why won’t politicians do anything?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/opinion/sunday/the-return-of-londons-fog.html', 'snippet': 'The city’s famous air pollution is coming back. So why won’t politicians do anything?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cambridge, England — IN January, researchers at King’s College London announced that pollution levels on Oxford Street, in central London, had exceeded limits set for the entire year in just the first four days of 2015. Similarly alarming numbers have been recorded for other streets in the city — and yet the mayor, Boris Johnson, has delayed implementation of stricter air-quality measures until 2020.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/opinion/sunday/08corton/08corton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 445, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/opinion/sunday/08corton/08corton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 445}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/opinion/sunday/08corton/08corton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 760, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/opinion/sunday/08corton/08corton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1520, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/opinion/sunday/08corton/08corton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/opinion/sunday/08corton/08corton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/opinion/sunday/08corton/08corton-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Return of London’s Fog', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Return of London’s Fog', 'name': N