f_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/746cea3d-f4b1-59de-bc4a-d683b4832fee', 'word_count': 979, 'uri': 'nyt://article/746cea3d-f4b1-59de-bc4a-d683b4832fee'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir and a story collection explore guarded and uprooted lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/hotel-and-vertigo-by-joanna-walsh.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir and a story collection explore guarded and uprooted lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I recently encountered a new nonfiction term: “the constructed I.” A quick consideration of any classic nonfiction text — one by Joan Didion, say — yields that the “I” in essays, journalism and memoirs is usually a partial invention. A nonfiction writer artfully sculptures an “I” narrator, similar to the way fiction writers sculpture their narrators. But what happens when a narrator is too constructed? When does artfulness ossify into artifice?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Julavitz/1108-BKS-Julavitz-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 306, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Julavitz/1108-BKS-Julavitz-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 306}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Julavitz/1108-BKS-Julavitz-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 521, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Julavitz/1108-BKS-Julavitz-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1043, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'cred