s ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Activision Blizzard Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kotick, Robert A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'King Digital Entertainment PLC', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4f8cd34-59d3-5c17-a42a-e4e23542887f', 'word_count': 382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4f8cd34-59d3-5c17-a42a-e4e23542887f'}, {'abstract': 'An examination of conventional wisdom about world hunger’s causes, and its future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/the-reproach-of-hunger-by-david-rieff.html', 'snippet': 'An examination of conventional wisdom about world hunger’s causes, and its future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hunger is probably the most salient consequence of poverty. This salience has some rationale: Hunger makes food a priority, and poor people spend about half of their income on it. But hunger is, nevertheless, a symptom of poverty, not a problem to be tackled in isolation — the solution to hunger may not have much to do with the supply of food. In this rambling book, David Rieff never quite recognizes this fundamental point. Positing that the 2008 spike in global food prices marked the start of structurally higher prices, his discussion revolves around whether we should look for the solution of extra food production in technocratic philanthropy, global agribusiness or revolutionary politics. Arriving at an answer that none look promising, he asks, “Where does this leave us?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08COLLIER/08COLLIER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08COLLIER/08COLLIER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08COLLIER/08COLLIER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08COLLIER/08COLLIER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08COLLIER/08COLLIER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08COLLIER/08COLLIER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08COLLIER/08COLLIER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Reproach of Hunger,’ by David Rieff', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Starving for Solutions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rieff, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Reproach of Hunger: Food, Justice, and Money in the Twenty-First Century (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Collier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/84cd6340-6fb5-5b26-9ccf-d6b5387cc85a', 'word_count': 668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84cd6340-6fb5-5b26-9ccf-d6b5387cc85a'}, {'abstract': 'A homeless Tanzanian boy leaves Dar es Salaam for the unknown wilderness in this debut novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/kid-moses-by-mark-r-thornton.html', 'snippet': 'A homeless Tanzanian boy leaves Dar es Salaam for the unknown wilderness in this debut novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the English-speaking world, “safari” evokes gaggles of sunburned tourists in pith helmets, gawking at lions and \xadelephants from the safety of open-topped all-terrain vehicles before ending the day with gin and tonics and Instagrams of acacia trees silhouetted against fiery African sunsets. In Swahili, “safari” just means a trip, a plain old trip of any variety.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Kid Moses,’ by Mark R. Thornton', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Into the Wild', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kulish, Nicholas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thornton, Mark R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Kid Moses (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kulish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kulish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a45e33a0-1110-5174-9b45-1e763f91519b', 'word_count': 647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a45e33a0-1110-5174-9b45-1e763f91519b'}, {'abstract': 'Authors with Russian roots discuss why Putin might appeal to novelists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/putin-reimagined.html', 'snippet': 'Authors with Russian roots discuss why Putin might appeal to novelists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, we look at two new books about Vladimir Putin and Russia under his rule (“The New Tsar” and “Winter Is Coming.”). Putin has also inspired fiction writers, appearing as himself (or in thin disguise) in books by Charles Cumming, Martin Cruz Smith and others. I asked authors with Russian roots why Putin might appeal to novelists.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-OpenBook/1108-BKS-OpenBook-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 261, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-OpenBook/1108-BKS-OpenBook-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 261}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-OpenBook/1108-BKS-OpenBook-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 445, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-OpenBook/1108-BKS-OpenBook-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 890, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-OpenBook/1108-BKS-OpenBook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-OpenBook/1108-BKS-OpenBook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-OpenBook/1108-BKS-OpenBook-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Putin, Reimagined', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Putin, Reimagined', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abd95400-cd7c-5e46-8ee5-fea865042616', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abd95400-cd7c-5e46-8ee5-fea865042616'}, {'abstract': 'A family rises to the challenge when their son insists he is a girl.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/becoming-nicole-by-amy-ellis-nutt.html', 'snippet': 'A family rises to the challenge when their son insists he is a girl.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Becoming Nicole: The Transformation of an American Family,” by Amy Ellis Nutt, generously traces the parameters of parental love. It tells the story of Wayne and Kelly Maines, a good-hearted, hard-working American couple, neither rich nor poor, neither fabulous nor downtrodden, who after years of trying to conceive a baby are finally given the opportunity to adopt: infant boys, identical twins, the children of Kelly’s second cousin. The Maineses name the babies Jonas and Wyatt, and immediately the low expectations that come with unmet yearning give way to the more self-centered aspirations that accompany getting what you want. Fantasies of imprinting upon these inchoate beings a storybook childhood preoccupy both parents, but especially Wayne, who sees in fatherhood a chance at passing down the rites and traditions of American manhood. Still in the hospital, Wayne cradles the babies and murmurs to them as if reciting a prayer: My boys, my boys. “We’re going to have so much fun together,” he whispers in the twins’ ears. “We’re going to go hunting and fishing and I will teach you everything I know.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Miller/1108-BKS-Miller-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 489, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Miller/1108-BKS-Miller-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 489}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Miller/1108-BKS-Miller-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Miller/1108-BKS-Miller-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1148, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Miller/1108-BKS-Miller-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Miller/1108-BKS-Miller-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Miller/1108-BKS-Miller-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Becoming Nicole,’ by Amy Ellis Nutt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coming Into Her Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Lisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nutt, Amy Ellis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Becoming Nicole: The Transformation of an American Family (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2e91cbe-0f89-51bf-8bbd-77c28f876f22', 'word_count': 1412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2e91cbe-0f89-51bf-8bbd-77c28f876f22'}, {'abstract': 'Look for your life: Two Latina writers explore the experience of finding their own way among multiple cultures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/sonia-manzanos-becoming-maria-and-margarita-engles-enchanted-air.html', 'snippet': 'Look for your life: Two Latina writers explore the experience of finding their own way among multiple cultures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the very first sentence, Margarita Engle’s memoir “Enchanted Air” takes wing. “When my parents met, it was love at first sight,” Engle writes. “They were standing on the terrace of an art school in an elegant palace now known as the Museo Romántico, the Romantic Museum.” Since her Cuban mother and American father did not speak the same language, they “communicated by passing drawings back and forth, like children in the back of a classroom. .\u2008.\u2008. Sketches, signs and gestures had to substitute for words.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Chambers01/1108-BKS-Chambers01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Chambers01/1108-BKS-Chambers01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 922}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Chambers01/1108-BKS-Chambers01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 667, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Chambers01/1108-BKS-Chambers01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1334, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Chambers01/1108-BKS-Chambers01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Chambers01/1108-BKS-Chambers01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-Chambers01/1108-BKS-Chambers01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sonia Manzano’s ‘Becoming Maria’ and Margarita Engle’s ‘Enchanted Air’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Búscate la Vida', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chambers, Veronica', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manzano, Sonia (1950- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Engle, Margarita (1951- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Enchanted Air: Two Cultures, Two Wings: A Memoir (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Becoming Maria: Love and Chaos in the South Bronx (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Veronica Chambers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Veronica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chambers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1527d7c3-9e0d-5b94-bb59-d91bbb57253c', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1527d7c3-9e0d-5b94-bb59-d91bbb57253c'}, {'abstract': 'In two books, biracial girls face an absent father, a cross-country move and the bigotry of those around them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/dream-on-amber-and-full-cicada-moon.html', 'snippet': 'In two books, biracial girls face an absent father, a cross-country move and the bigotry of those around them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Children’s literature has been engulfed in a conversation on the lack of books featuring minority characters. This call for diversity has been accompanied by uncomfortable yet necessary debates about what constitutes quality representation, and few people agree on that. Two new novels about biracial girls illustrate the range of approaches authors can take to these issues. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Dream On, Amber’ and ‘Full Cicada Moon’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Multi-Something', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hilton, Marilyn (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shevah, Emma', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dream On, Amber (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Full Cicada Moon (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malinda Lo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malinda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/32457edb-bdfe-52d1-8a4a-787872b4ac1f', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32457edb-bdfe-52d1-8a4a-787872b4ac1f'}, {'abstract': 'Imaginary sidekicks help their picture-book friends through tight spots and tough times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/philip-c-steads-lenny-lucy-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Imaginary sidekicks help their picture-book friends through tight spots and tough times.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It is through fantasy that children achieve catharsis,” Maurice Sendak said when he accepted the Caldecott Medal for “Where the Wild Things Are” in 1964. “It is the best means they have for taming Wild Things.” In four new picture books about imaginary friends, conjured companions of all kinds help children confront the beastly parts of life. In these books, the children’s situations change when they meet real-life friends, but reassuringly, their chimerical pals never disappear.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/1108-BKS-RABB1/1108-BKS-RABB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 547, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/1108-BKS-RABB1/1108-BKS-RABB1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 547}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/1108-BKS-RABB1/1108-BKS-RABB1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 933, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/1108-BKS-RABB1/1108-BKS-RABB1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1866, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/1108-BKS-RABB1/1108-BKS-RABB1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/1108-BKS-RABB1/1108-BKS-RABB1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/1108-BKS-RABB1/1108-BKS-RABB1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Philip C. Stead’s ‘Lenny & Lucy,’ and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Now You See Them', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colfer, Eoin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van de Vendel, Edward (1964- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodrich, Carter (1959- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stead, Philip Christian (1981- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stead, Erin E', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeffers, Oliver', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Van Hertbruggen, Anton (1990- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Imaginary Fred (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Lenny & Lucy (Book)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'We Forgot Brock! (Book)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Dog That Nino Didn't Have (Book)", 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margo Rabb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rabb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/424e41a8-3225-5b58-be61-8b6bb4c9b5c7', 'word_count': 970, 'uri': 'nyt://article/424e41a8-3225-5b58-be61-8b6bb4c9b5c7'}, {'abstract': 'Escaping a brutal home, this protagonist models herself on inspiring fictional characters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/the-hired-girl-by-laura-amy-schlitz.html', 'snippet': 'Escaping a brutal home, this protagonist models herself on inspiring fictional characters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Surrounded by a brutish father and brothers and aching for her dead mother, 14-year-old Joan Skraggs runs away from her family’s Pennsylvania farm, makes her way to Baltimore, and is rescued from a park bench by a well-dressed young man. She presents herself as Janet Lovelace, age 18, and becomes “the hired girl,” a servant to the Rosenbachs — a prosperous Jewish family who own a department store. It’s 1911, the tail end of the Gilded Age, and world wars have yet to break out. Janet keeps a diary, and the reader quickly gleans that her notions of life come from three books: “Jane Eyre,” “Dombey and Son” and “Ivanhoe.” “I plan to go on as bravely as a heroine in a novel,” she writes. “In my new life I’m not going to be vulgar. Even though I’m going to be a servant I’m going to cultivate my finer feelings. I will better myself and write with truth and refinement.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Hired Girl,’ by Laura Amy Schlitz', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘As Bravely as a Heroine in a Novel’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schlitz, Laura Amy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Hired Girl (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lenora Todaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lenora', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Todaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/905e6615-3f13-5e43-80b4-2c6cf58c02eb', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/905e6615-3f13-5e43-80b4-2c6cf58c02eb'}, {'abstract': 'A novel plays with Y.A. formulas, keeping the supernaturally special teenagers in the background. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/the-rest-of-us-just-live-here-by-patrick-ness.html', 'snippet': 'A novel plays with Y.A. formulas, keeping the supernaturally special teenagers in the background. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'At my typical public high school in an average-size town, I was surrounded by a lot of kids just like me — that is to say, no one had any special powers. There were no vampires. No witches. No werewolves or shape shifters or zombie fighters. No one was on a mission to save the world from the forces of evil. Mostly, we were learning to drive cars, trying to pass calculus, angling for the first glimmerings of what adult relationships might hold. We were teenagers, for all the beauty and mundanity in that. It was horrible, fantastic, brutal, magical and sometimes utterly boring. It was, in a word, life.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Rest of Us Just Live Here,’ by Patrick Ness', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Everyday Saviors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doll, Jen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ness, Patrick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Rest of Us Just Live Here (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jen Doll', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doll', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f75185e2-8407-565b-800a-127f75ab3735', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f75185e2-8407-565b-800a-127f75ab3735'}, {'abstract': 'A novel from the author of the Percy Jackson series uses a boy’s adventure to enter the world of Norse mythology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/rick-riordans-magnus-chase-and-the-gods-of-asgard.html', 'snippet': 'A novel from the author of the Percy Jackson series uses a boy’s adventure to enter the world of Norse mythology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The line between cynical repetition and elegant variation is as fine in adventure stories as it is in — well, as it is in that line the members of Spinal Tap sapiently discovered between clever and stupid. Rick Riordan, after the well-earned triumph of his two Percy Jackson series, tales of an all-American boy who discovers that he is the son of a Greek god, now embarks, with “Magnus Chase and the Gods of Asgard: The Sword of Summer,” on an entirely new series, this one about a not-quite-all-American boy who discovers he is the son of .\u2008.\u2008. a Norse god. The boys are certainly different \xad— Magnus, our new hero, a Kurt Cobain look-alike, starts out homeless and a petty thief in Boston, where Percy was at boarding school.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08GOPNIK/08GOPNIK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08GOPNIK/08GOPNIK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 771}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08GOPNIK/08GOPNIK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 798, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08GOPNIK/08GOPNIK-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1788, 'width': 1392, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08GOPNIK/08GOPNIK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08GOPNIK/08GOPNIK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/08GOPNIK/08GOPNIK-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rick Riordan’s ‘Magnus Chase and the Gods of Asgard’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Gods Live in Boston', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riordan, Rick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Magnus Chase and the Gods of Asgard: The Sword of Summer (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Gopnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gopnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a3d0ed8-20a7-5c77-acb1-d09bf6237a5b', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a3d0ed8-20a7-5c77-acb1-d09bf6237a5b'}, {'abstract': 'A girl whose compromised immune system has kept her isolated decides to take a risk for love in this young adult novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/everything-everything-by-nicola-yoon.html', 'snippet': 'A girl whose compromised immune system has kept her isolated decides to take a risk for love in this young adult novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even if you knew love could kill you, what would you risk to experience it — just once? Just for a little while? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Everything, Everything,’ by Nicola Yoon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Girl in a Bubble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yoon, Nicola (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yoon, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Everything, Everything (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Whitney Joiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Whitney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9da1dd54-6cbd-53b9-b037-d9dc5dc7982d', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9da1dd54-6cbd-53b9-b037-d9dc5dc7982d'}, {'abstract': 'Racism, love and friendship power a story that begins with a girl’s pain and ends in a town’s tragedy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/out-of-darkness-by-ashley-hope-perez.html', 'snippet': 'Racism, love and friendship power a story that begins with a girl’s pain and ends in a town’s tragedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The novels that burn into our minds as young adults often involve horror of some kind. A mere mention of “All Quiet on the Western Front” or “I Am the Cheese,” and I’m right back to brooding again in the ocean-deep emotions of my adolescence. What is that urge, the desire to soak in anguish and injustice as we come of age? Whatever it is, Ashley Hope Pérez’s new novel, “Out of Darkness,” fills the need. Her layered tale of color lines, love and struggle in an East Texas oil town is a pit-in-the-stomach family drama that goes down like it should, with pain and fascination, like a mix of sugary medicine and artisanal moonshine. I actually had to close the book at one point to seek respite with Facebook. And puppies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Out of Darkness,’ by Ashley Hope Pérez', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Color Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cave, Damien', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perez, Ashley Hope (1984- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Out of Darkness (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damien Cave', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cave', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/37e9bad1-93bc-56d5-81a8-7e365cd7b03d', 'word_count': 754, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37e9bad1-93bc-56d5-81a8-7e365cd7b03d'}, {'abstract': 'A middle-grade novel invents a spy network of children so ordinary they can carry out their mission without being noticed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/the-league-of-unexceptional-children-by-gitty-daneshvari.html', 'snippet': 'A middle-grade novel invents a spy network of children so ordinary they can carry out their mission without being noticed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cloak of American exceptionalism wears some permanent stains down its front — like slavery and the denial of civil rights. And fresh stains, too, like exaggerated income inequality. Likewise, the reality of the elite, exceptional American child is hardly as pristine as many parents imagine. Our “gifted and talented” Mandarin-speaking, travel-team-\xadcaptaining, polished-in-selfies youth are often characterized by narcissism, the pursuit of achievement at any cost, toxic stress and greed: grade greed, accomplishment greed, résumé greed. Precocious superstardom is not always happy. And regular, average childhood is not necessarily a problem that needs fixing. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The League of Unexceptional Children,’ by\xa0Gitty Daneshvari', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Regular Kids', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daneshvari, Gitty (1978- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The League of Unexceptional Children (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Renee Dale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Renee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d2cc670-964e-57ea-90c6-43f192a7039d', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d2cc670-964e-57ea-90c6-43f192a7039d'}, {'abstract': 'Three illustrated books for a broad range of ages find ways to shine new light on classic fairy tales.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/vasilisa-the-beautiful-retold-by-anthea-bell-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three illustrated books for a broad range of ages find ways to shine new light on classic fairy tales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fairy tale retellings: Climb up a pile of these revamped, remixed and rehabilitated classics and we reach high enough to wake the giant. And yet, no matter how much we empower the princess, humanize the witch and emasculate the prince, or how earnestly we whack and whittle these tales to reflect us, we seem only to make the original tales stronger. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-CHAINANI2/1108-BKS-CHAINANI2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-CHAINANI2/1108-BKS-CHAINANI2-articleLarge-v2.jp