ue': 'Morgunova, Anna', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Sleeper and the Spindle (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Vasilisa the Beautiful (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Most Wonderful Thing in the World (Book)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Soman Chainani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Soman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chainani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/85faf87f-1c4b-5d94-a5ad-f1e1b077ec6c', 'word_count': 1054, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85faf87f-1c4b-5d94-a5ad-f1e1b077ec6c'}, {'abstract': 'After an assault, four high school students, two with mental disabilities, become linked by universal emotions and challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/a-step-toward-falling-by-cammie-mcgovern.html', 'snippet': 'After an assault, four high school students, two with mental disabilities, become linked by universal emotions and challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cammie McGovern’s “A Step Toward Falling” opens with two high school seniors, Emily and Lucas, taking on 40 hours of community service at a center for young adults with disabilities. Emily and Lucas will serve as models for “a typical peer’s approach to friendship and dating” in a class called Boundaries and Relationships. They are not there by choice.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Step Toward Falling,’ by Cammie McGovern', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Do the Right Thing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGovern, Cammie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Step Toward Falling (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Priscilla Gilman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Priscilla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gilman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9889b440-2983-573a-a7b9-f89772a10419', 'word_count': 665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9889b440-2983-573a-a7b9-f89772a10419'}, {'abstract': 'From toys left to their own devices to an enchanted hare to a zooful of playfellows, flights of flakes lead to flights of fancy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/the-snow-rabbit-by-camille-garoche-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'From toys left to their own devices to an enchanted hare to a zooful of playfellows, flights of flakes lead to flights of fancy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is the secret to snow’s alchemy — its power to transform any ordinary street into its own world? Backyards, driveways and rooftops take cover under white and everything is changed. Snow means gloves and boots and anticipation. It doubles your speed downhill, then cushions your fall at the bottom. It makes it much harder for school buses to reach their destination. But as three new books show, our captivation lies in something more than the break from routine and the opportunity for daredevil stunts. We make snowmen, we make angels, we build fortresses and we leave our footsteps wherever we go. After it snows, we sculpture the world around us, carving out stages upon which our imaginations perform. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-NEAL2/1108-BKS-NEAL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-NEAL2/1108-BKS-NEAL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-NEAL2/1108-BKS-NEAL2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-NEAL2/1108-BKS-NEAL2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1268, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-NEAL2/1108-BKS-NEAL2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-NEAL2/1108-BKS-NEAL2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-NEAL2/1108-BKS-NEAL2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Snow Rabbit,’ by Camille Garoche, and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Winter’s Tales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garoche, Camille', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jenkins, Emily', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Usher, Sam (1984- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zelinsky, Paul O', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Snow (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Snow Rabbit (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Toys Meet Snow (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Silas Neal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'Silas', 'lastname': 'Neal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfe73c1a-2bf7-59c8-ba37-0040efe25825', 'word_count': 990, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfe73c1a-2bf7-59c8-ba37-0040efe25825'}, {'abstract': 'An account of Daniel Ellsberg and his release of the Pentagon Papers introduces young readers to the Vietnam War. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/steve-sheinkins-most-dangerous.html', 'snippet': 'An account of Daniel Ellsberg and his release of the Pentagon Papers introduces young readers to the Vietnam War. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'About 70 pages into his gripping new history for young readers, “Most Dangerous: Daniel Ellsberg and the Secret History of the Vietnam War,” Steve Sheinkin seems to switch genres, describing a scene that could have been taken from “The Walking Dead”: a trailer full of corpses, its floor “streaked with blood and brains.” Arms and legs were falling off the rotting trunks, which made it difficult to count how many bodies were in the trailer. The stench was unbearable. So the bodies were hosed down and the trailer tipped to its side, letting, as one witness put it, a “rivulet of blood-colored water” flow outside. A delegation of American military officers passed by, stepping over the blood “to avoid ruining the shine on their boots.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Steve Sheinkin’s ‘Most Dangerous’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Good Morning, Vietnam', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grandin, Greg', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheinkin, Steve', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellsberg, Daniel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Most Dangerous: Daniel Ellsberg and the Secret History of the Vietnam War (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Grandin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grandin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5477c129-c96d-5190-93c8-388a45092151', 'word_count': 695, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5477c129-c96d-5190-93c8-388a45092151'}, {'abstract': '“Depraved Heart,” Patricia Cornwell’s 23rd mystery about the medical examiner Kay Scarpetta, enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 3.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': '“Depraved Heart,” Patricia Cornwell’s 23rd mystery about the medical examiner Kay Scarpetta, enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 3.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fight or Flight: “Depraved Heart,” Patricia Cornwell’s 23rd mystery about the medical examiner Kay Scarpetta — Kirkus Reviews called it “terse and tangled, messy and body-fluid-y and altogether satisfying” — enters the hardcover fiction list at No.\u202f3. In a profile last year, The Telegraph noted that Cornwell has “the coiled-up, fight-or-flight air of a lynx about to spring,” a trait evident on her website’s home page, where a video shows her coolly handling an MP5 submachine gun. “I can’t imagine writing about a certain type of rifle or a type of shotgun or a certain handgun for anything, and not having any experience with it,” she explains in the video. Cornwell has been known to take her research to extremes: She spent six years in a forensics lab to learn Scarpetta’s job and went on to scuba dive, fly a helicopter and stab sides of beef wrapped in wool, all in the name of getting things right. “People now have such short attention spans that I have to try really hard to keep the suspense going, so they don’t have a reason to put down my book and read 20,000 tweets instead,” Cornwell told The Telegraph. “Crime novels are a great excuse to get people together and to .\u2008.\u2008. examine issues that affect us all,” she added. “The crime is just the party to get the people to show up.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/books/review/05cornwall/05cornwall-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/books/review/05cornwall/05cornwall-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 452, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/books/review/05cornwall/05cornwall-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/05/books/review/05cornwall/05cornwall-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/05/books/review/05cornwall/05cornwall-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-06T18:15:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5956eead-55f1-5999-ae8e-4906c4493ccd', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5956eead-55f1-5999-ae8e-4906c4493ccd'}, {'abstract': 'From conflict to confederation, three new picture books conjure portions of the past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/books/review/don-browns-aaron-and-alexander-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'From conflict to confederation, three new picture books conjure portions of the past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once a lion and a man were walking, and they saw a great bronze sculpture on a pedestal. A bronze man was locked in battle with a fearsome bronze lion. The bronze man had pinned the bronze lion to the base of the pedestal and was clearly about to win the fight. The man said to the lion, “What a battle that must have been, can you imagine that, a man beating a lion?” The lion said to the man, “No, I can’t, but I can imagine that men are much better at making bronze \xadsculptures.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-MYERS2/1108-BKS-MYERS2-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 183, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-MYERS2/1108-BKS-MYERS2-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 183}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-MYERS2/1108-BKS-MYERS2-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 312, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-MYERS2/1108-BKS-MYERS2-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 1313, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-MYERS2/1108-BKS-MYERS2-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-MYERS2/1108-BKS-MYERS2-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/books/review/1108-BKS-MYERS2/1108-BKS-MYERS2-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don Brown’s ‘Aaron and Alexander,’ and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Along the Nation’s Way', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': N