ousands of household items preserved in the crowded apartments of the Lower East Side, including curry powder nearly a century old and jars of morphine-laced medicine.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I think most people associate the historic immigrant experience first with Ellis Island,” said David Favaloro, a curator at the Tenement Museum on the Lower East Side, “and then specifically with this neighborhood.” The museum occupies a building at 97 Orchard Street, a five-floor walk-up that was home to some 7,000 immigrants (of mostly German and Irish descent) from 1863 to 1935, when it lost its residential designation amid a modernization of the city’s anarchic fire codes.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08VAULT1SUB/08VAULT1SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08VAULT1SUB/08VAULT1SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 688}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08VAULT1SUB/08VAULT1SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 894, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08VAULT1SUB/08VAULT1SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1787, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08VAULT1SUB/08VAULT1SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08VAULT1SUB/08VAULT1SUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwid