le': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/27a54d80-f09c-5331-a928-3cb576453981', 'word_count': 919, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27a54d80-f09c-5331-a928-3cb576453981'}, {'abstract': 'The perception that the current mayor too easily capitulates to real estate interests, that he cares little about the hazards of extreme density and lacks any vision for urban planning, is very real.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/08/nyregion/development-de-blasio-style.html', 'snippet': 'The perception that the current mayor too easily capitulates to real estate interests, that he cares little about the hazards of extreme density and lacks any vision for urban planning, is very real.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Lynn Ellsworth moved to New York in the 1970s, before she went on to get a doctorate in economics, she lived in a boardinghouse on the Upper West Side, with a dozen or so young women who were students at Barnard and Parsons. The system was vibrant and efficient; this was high-density living in a low-density neighborhood. At some point during more recent years, the buildings on her old block in the West 80s were converted into single-family townhouses. In the intervening period, during the 1990s, Ms. Ellsworth moved to TriBeCa, where the frenetically paced construction of expensive high-rise condominiums — high-density urbanism of a far less romantic kind — has been troublesome and disorienting.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08BIG/08BIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/08/nyregion/08BIG/08BIG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 437}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None,