'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcc1a581-d916-545e-9b4a-6cbf58c751ff', 'word_count': 985, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcc1a581-d916-545e-9b4a-6cbf58c751ff'}, {'abstract': 'As MediaStorm celebrates 10 years, its founder looks ahead to the challenges and opportunities facing photographers in the online world.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/10/the-killer-app-storytelling/', 'snippet': 'As MediaStorm celebrates 10 years, its founder looks ahead to the challenges and opportunities facing photographers in the online world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brian Storm was raised in the Detroit area and attended the College of the Ozarks on a baseball scholarship. While obtaining a graduate degree at the University of Missouri’s School of Journalism in 1993, he discovered computers and began making interactive CD-ROM disks. He started a company, MediaStorm, but put it on hold while working for MSNBC and Corbis over the next decade.He relaunched MediaStorm in 2005 as a film-production company and interactive design studio, which this month celebrates its 10th anniversary. He spoke with James Estrin about the future of photography and multimedia. Their conversation has been edited.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Killer App: Storytelling', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Storm, Brian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MediaStorm LLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-10T10:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59fca0b4-a394-5027-9958-08fd7e934c90', 'word_count': 1825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59fca0b4-a394-5027-9958-08fd7e934c90'}, {'abstract': 'Roper’s book argues that Nabokov’s American period, from 1940 to 1960, was beneficial to the author in every way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/nabokov-in-america-by-robert-roper.html', 'snippet': 'Roper’s book argues that Nabokov’s American period, from 1940 to 1960, was beneficial to the author in every way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who would have thought there’d be more to say about Vladimir Nabokov? This most scholarly and mandarin of writers, an incontestable master of his craft, has been parsed and chronicled to death — the subject of Brian Boyd’s two-\xadvolume biography as well as Stacy Schiff’s portrait of a marriage (“Véra: Mrs. Vladimir Nabokov”), not to mention thousands of critical studies. So my first thought when I set eyes on Robert Roper’s “Nabokov in America: On the Road to ‘Lolita’\u200a” was, “Is this book necessary?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15MERKIN/15MERKIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 454, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15MERKIN/15MERKIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 454}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15MERKIN/15MERKIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 775, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15MERKIN/15MERKIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1549, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15MERKIN/15MERKIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15MERKIN/15MERKIN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15MERKIN/15MERKIN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Nabokov in America,’ by Robert Roper', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Nabokov in America,’ by Robert Roper', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roper, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nabokov, Vladimir', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Nabokov in America: On the Road to Lolita (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-10T10:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daphne Merkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daphne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Merkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f2984ea-11ed-5a3e-b579-b2327bee8cd9', 'word_count': 1375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f2984ea-11ed-5a3e-b579-b2327bee8cd9'}, {'abstract': 'Nabokov’s letters to Véra suggest a sky-filling \xadadoration and a helpless dependency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/in-letters-to-vera-vladimir-nabokov-writes-to-his-wife.html', 'snippet': 'Nabokov’s letters to Véra suggest a sky-filling \xadadoration and a helpless dependency.', 'lead_paragraph': ' My sun, my soul, my song, my bird, my sweetheart, my pink sky, my sunny rainbow, my little music, my inexpressible delight, my softness, my tenderness, my lightness, my dear life, my dear eyes. .\u2008.\u2008. These endearments and salutations (backed up by a crowded menagerie of surrogates: Goosikins, Poochums, Tigercubkin, Puppykin) suggest a sky-filling \xadadoration and, more than that, a helpless dependency. As early as Vladimir Nabokov’s second letter to Véra Slonim, after a couple of months of chaste acquaintanceship, he lays it all before her: “I cannot write a word without hearing how you will pronounce it — and can’t recall a single trifle I’ve lived through without regret — so sharp! — that we haven’t lived through it together. .\u2008.\u2008. You came into my life .\u2008.\u2008. as one comes to a kingdom where all the rivers have been waiting for your reflection, all the roads, for your steps.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15COVER/15COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 476, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15COVER/15COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 476}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15COVER/15COVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub