, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-10T18:35:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bba6f3c-e019-54c7-97da-bdc9397018a9', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bba6f3c-e019-54c7-97da-bdc9397018a9'}, {'abstract': 'Combina will feature the chef’s trademark Israeli-Middle Eastern fare; Arepa Factory opens in the East Village; Quality Meats expands; and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/11/dining/einat-admonys-new-restaurant-set-to-open.html', 'snippet': 'Combina will feature the chef’s trademark Israeli-Middle Eastern fare; Arepa Factory opens in the East Village; Quality Meats expands; and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Combina The chef Einat Admony’s latest restaurant, a wide room with an open kitchen and bar and an expanse of windows, will feature her trademark Israeli-Middle Eastern fare, but often with a bright Spanish inflection. The haloumi (cheese) comes dressed with chimichurri; a burger is made with merguez, the North African sausage; fried eggplant comes on a tostada; and there are salt cod doughnuts. “Not fritters, real doughnuts,” Ms. Admony said. The drinks list includes sherries, ciders, vermouths and homemade limoncello made with Moroccan-style preserved lemons. Joining her as chef in this venture is Molly Breidenthal. Lacking basement storage, the owners installed shelving all around to hold ingredients. (Opens Saturday): 330 West Broadway (Grand Street), 212-226-1248.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/11/dining/11OFF/11OFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subT