:06:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Martin and Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/852c75ae-134b-5628-b264-122f96096f8e', 'word_count': 1800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/852c75ae-134b-5628-b264-122f96096f8e'}, {'abstract': 'The mayor said the city would dump about 2.1 billion gallons of raw sewage into the St. Lawrence River.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/11/world/americas/canada-montreal-to-dump-sewage-into-river-during-repair-work.html', 'snippet': 'The mayor said the city would dump about 2.1 billion gallons of raw sewage into the St. Lawrence River.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mayor of Montreal, Denis Coderre, said Tuesday that the city would begin dumping about 2.1 billion gallons of raw sewage into the St. Lawrence River early Wednesday. The diversion, which may continue for up to seven days, will allow workers to repair and alter a major collector sewer. “If we could have avoided this choice, we would have done it,” Mr. Coderre told reporters. After initially approving the city’s plan, the former Conservative federal government banned the city from diverting the sewage in the midst of the recent election campaign. The Conservatives were routed by the Liberal Party last month. The new Liberal government said Monday that the diversion could go ahead, provided that the city addressed concerns raised by a panel of scientists. That review found that while dumping the sewage was not ideal, it was preferable to the potential consequences of a sewer failure. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canada: Montreal to Dump Sewage Into River During Repair Work', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Americas; Canada: Montreal to Dump Sewage Into River During Repair Work', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Ian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montreal (Quebec)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Conservative Party (Canada)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Liberal Party (Canada)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coderre, Denis (1962- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-11T04:10:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5570a69a-8359-5423-8da1-6596676893de', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5570a69a-8359-5423-8da1-6596676893de'}, {'abstract': 'The billionaire is wearing thin, the scion is fading away and the youngsters have the thunder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/11/opinion/trouble-for-bush-and-trump.html', 'snippet': 'The billionaire is wearing thin, the scion is fading away and the youngsters have the thunder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Going into the latest debate, the trending question about Jeb Bush on Google was whether he was “still running for president.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': 