', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ex-Convicts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-11T08:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b627bfb-e983-54bf-8211-80045fc0bba0', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b627bfb-e983-54bf-8211-80045fc0bba0'}, {'abstract': 'Stronger safeguards are needed to prevent governments from using the agency’s list of fugitives to persecute dissidents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/11/opinion/the-misuse-of-interpols-database.html', 'snippet': 'Stronger safeguards are needed to prevent governments from using the agency’s list of fugitives to persecute dissidents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fugitive alerts issued by Interpol, the international law enforcement clearinghouse, can make it hard for fugitives to slip across borders. But the group’s “red notice” database, which can be reviewed by authorities across the world to identify people facing prosecution, is frequently used by authoritarian governments to control and persecute dissidents, human rights activists and journalists.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/11/opinion/11wed1web/11wed1web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/11/opinion/11wed1web/11wed1web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/11/opinion/11wed1web/11wed1web-jumbo.jpg', 'he