 Makari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Makari', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f24c8d14-7d55-5fca-aff7-b12896a043df', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f24c8d14-7d55-5fca-aff7-b12896a043df'}, {'abstract': 'The dancer Mina Nishimura was the unheralded guest who appeared onstage alongside Sia on “Saturday Night Live.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/11/arts/television/sia-and-her-terpsichorean-shadow.html', 'snippet': 'The dancer Mina Nishimura was the unheralded guest who appeared onstage alongside Sia on “Saturday Night Live.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who was that lithe, bewigged doppelgänger dancing next to Sia on “Saturday Night Live” this past weekend? In “Alive,” she was a karate-kicking warrior; in “Bird Set Free,” she escaped from a sheath of fabric, writhing on the floor and rising to discover her dancing voice. She wasn’t, as Rolling Stone described, a “young martial artist,” but the Butoh-trained Japanese dancer Mina Nishimura, an important presence on New York’s downtown dance scene who is still in shock about the experience of performing on television. She sees herself, she said, as a “quirky starfish, which draws attention from only a few certain people.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/11/arts/11nishimurasnl/11nishimurasnl-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/11/arts/11nishimurasnl/11nishimurasnl-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/11/arts/11nishimurasnl/11nishimurasnl-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jum