e': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Easton (Conn)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-11T15:42:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Real Estate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0d66299d-d0b2-500c-bd8d-2532d7f123b6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0d66299d-d0b2-500c-bd8d-2532d7f123b6'}, {'abstract': 'The Fairfield County suburb is home to at least 20 farms of varying sizes, from part-time specialty operations to large-scale agritourism attractions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/realestate/easton-conn-embracing-a-farming-culture.html', 'snippet': 'The Fairfield County suburb is home to at least 20 farms of varying sizes, from part-time specialty operations to large-scale agritourism attractions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Patti and Allan Popp didn’t move to the rural town of Easton, Conn., to take up farming. They were simply searching for more privacy than they had at their previous home in Stratford. Ms. Popp happened to fall in love with a 1740 house in foreclosure along one of Easton’s main routes, Sport Hill Road.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Easton, Conn.: Embracing a Farming Culture', 'kicker': 'Living In', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Embracing a Farming Culture ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prevost, Lisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Easton (Conn)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-11T15:42:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Prevost', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prevost', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/758e3984-13fc-52c1-99eb-723257af04b5', 'word_count': 1331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/758e3984-13fc-52c1-99eb-723257af04b5'}, {'abstract': 'For that worshiped album’s 50th-anniversary celebration, a complete masters version is due out Nov. 20. And the Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra will perform its arrangement of the suite.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/arts/music/coltranes-a-love-supreme-rereleased-and-celebrated-at-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': 'For that worshiped album’s 50th-anniversary celebration, a complete masters version is due out Nov. 20. And the Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra will perform its arrangement of the suite.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The saxophonist John Coltrane released his most revered album, “A Love Supreme,” on the Impulse! label in 1965, and from the start it was understood as something special: a four-part suite that drew Coltrane’s magnificent quartet into deep, devotional focus. The album has been reissued in a deluxe edition before, but a boxed set due out on Friday, Nov. 20, “A Love Supreme: The Complete Masters,” offers the deepest dive yet, with alternate takes, studio chatter, photo reproductions and a new essay by Ashley Kahn, who once wrote a book on the subject.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15GUIDE8/15GUIDE8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1046, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge':