Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61a39214-7185-5818-8fce-50b8dd52ee02', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61a39214-7185-5818-8fce-50b8dd52ee02'}, {'abstract': 'The exhibit “Coney Island: Visions of an American Dreamland, 1861-2008” presents works from artists like Diane Arbus and Joseph Stella.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/arts/design/coney-island-culture-at-the-brooklyn-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The exhibit “Coney Island: Visions of an American Dreamland, 1861-2008” presents works from artists like Diane Arbus and Joseph Stella.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is a microcosm of American popular culture in all its ersatz glory, the anti-Disneyland, a realm of tawdry spectacle and mindless distraction catering to the basic instincts of hoi polloi. It is, of course, Coney Island, whose history and culture are chronicled in “Coney Island: Visions of an American Dreamland, 1861-2008,” opening Friday, Nov. 20, at the Brooklyn Museum.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 997, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 997, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15GUIDE6/15GUIDE6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Coney Island Culture, at the Brooklyn Museum', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; Amusement for Everyone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMUSEMENT AND THEME PARKS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Coney Island (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARBUS, DIANE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chase, William Merritt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-11T15:45:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8d1da1d-8a96-5205-8d63-a78b0e57ab1a', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8d1da1d-8a96-5205-8d63-a78b0e57ab1a'}, {'abstract': 'The rapper talks about a range of influences he drew on — Amanda Blank, M.I.A., Dev Hynes and others — in addressing misogyny, homophobia and more in his first full-length album.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/arts/music/le1f-speaks-out-like-his-fearless-models-on-riot-boi.html', 'snippet': 'The rapper talks about a range of influences he drew on — Amanda Blank, M.I.A., Dev Hynes and others — in addressing misogyny, homophobia and more in his first full-length album.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On his first full-length album, “Riot Boi,” the New York rapper Le1f is playful, painfully clever, aloof, outraged and deadly serious, often in the span of a few lines. “Rats and roaches running all over the hood in the daytime,” he begins on Track 1, “but I still don’t see none of that in their raps, their rhymes or their headlines.” Topical issues covered across the 12 songs include homophobia, misogyny, transgender rights and Black Lives Matter. “You just wanna ignore it all, don’t you?” Le1f scoffs. “Basic.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15PLAYLIST1/15PLAYLIST1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15PLAYLIST1/15PLAYLIST1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15PLAYLIST1/15PLAYLIST1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15PLAYLIST1/15PLAYLIST1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1708, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt