: None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd9494b3-2425-53a7-91a9-3cffd259afcc', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd9494b3-2425-53a7-91a9-3cffd259afcc'}, {'abstract': 'It’s become an act of parental dereliction to let a child turn in a project that looks like a child’s work.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/11/when-a-childs-project-shows-a-parental-hand-at-work/', 'snippet': 'It’s become an act of parental dereliction to let a child turn in a project that looks like a child’s work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my younger son, Finn, was in third grade, his class staged a wax museum as the culmination of a biography project. Each student researched a historical figure they admired, prepared a monologue about that person’s life and historical significance, and on the day of the wax museum presentation, dressed up like their chosen hero. As no academic project is complete without the requisite trifold poster, each student also had to create an illustrated, informative display. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/11/us/finn-project-motherlode/finn-project-motherlode-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/11/us/finn-project-motherlode/finn-project-motherlode-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/11/us/finn-project-motherlode/finn-project-motherlode-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail'