inal': 'By David Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/58904334-6c6e-5395-834b-052451e38511', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58904334-6c6e-5395-834b-052451e38511'}, {'abstract': 'Abraham.In.Motion presents works inspired by jazz and hip-hop at the Joyce Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/12/arts/dance/review-kyle-abraham-sleekly-protesting-racial-injustice.html', 'snippet': 'Abraham.In.Motion presents works inspired by jazz and hip-hop at the Joyce Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Race and music are potent tools for Kyle Abraham, a Pittsburgh-born choreographer and dancer who grew up enmeshed in hip-hop culture. In his newest work, “Absent Matter,” Mr. Abraham takes inspiration from jazz and hip-hop to focus on racial injustice. It’s a timely — and, for a dance, a huge — subject. Yet despite his status as a 2013 MacArthur Fellow, Mr. Abraham is not fully formed enough as a choreographer to tackle it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/12/arts/ABRAHAM1/ABRAHAM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/12/arts/ABRAHAM1/ABRAHAM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/12/arts/ABRAHAM1/ABRAHAM1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/12/arts/ABRAHAM1/ABRAHAM1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width'