 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Atkinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4952536c-79af-5f95-920c-9096182b9bfc', 'word_count': 2157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4952536c-79af-5f95-920c-9096182b9bfc'}, {'abstract': 'Two fiction writers known for their mind-bending imaginations discuss the business of envisioning the future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/magazine/choose-your-own-adventure-a-conversation-with-jennifer-egan-and-george-saunders.html', 'snippet': 'Two fiction writers known for their mind-bending imaginations discuss the business of envisioning the future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jennifer Egan: I want to start by talking about how we define writing about the future. Some things that I think of as futuristic, when I’ve looked at them again for this conversation, I’m not sure they really are. So I guess my question would be: What leads you into territory that feels futuristic? How do you end up there? And what makes it the future?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '74', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/magazine/15dialogue1/15dialogue1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/magazine/15dialogue1/15dialogue1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/magazine/15dialogue1/15dialogue1-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 573, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/magazine/15dialogue1/15dialogue1-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1146, 'width':