'word_count': 1955, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12e3cebb-6487-5593-9271-7e71f9886f6a'}, {'abstract': 'This lavish show includes 160 objects from one of the most comprehensive collections of Japanese art in the West.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/13/arts/design/masterpieces-of-japanese-art-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': 'This lavish show includes 160 objects from one of the most comprehensive collections of Japanese art in the West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Has there ever been a more beautiful autumn than the one now on the wane in New York City, with our parks still looking like cloth-of-gold, and flocks of southbound geese calling over our rivers? We know there were just such seasons in centuries past in Japan. Artists tell us so. In a 16th-century hanging scroll, the painter Shikibu Terutada depicts fall leaves and flowers as last-chance blasts of color. In the 18th century, Maruyama Okyo fills an all-but-empty folding screen with the image of a single goose descending to withered reeds.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/13GRIGGS/13GRIGGS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/13GRIGGS/13GRIGGS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/13GRIGGS/13GRIGGS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/13GRIGGS/13GRIGGS-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None,