rticle', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kristin Hussey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kristin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hussey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fd132ab-a7b9-5b3c-9b47-7dcfc8a6cee1', 'word_count': 586, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fd132ab-a7b9-5b3c-9b47-7dcfc8a6cee1'}, {'abstract': 'The pianist performed Schumann’s “Scenes From Childhood” and gave a deft interpretation of Ravel’s “Miroirs."', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/13/arts/music/review-jean-yves-thibaudet-emotional-austerity-at-carnegie.html', 'snippet': 'The pianist performed Schumann’s “Scenes From Childhood” and gave a deft interpretation of Ravel’s “Miroirs."', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like other ostensibly “simple” piano works, such as Mozart sonatas and Bach’s two-part inventions, Schumann’s “Kinderszenen” (“Scenes From Childhood”) reveals much in terms of technique and expression. But when Jean-Yves Thibaudet performed it at Carnegie Hall on Wednesday evening, it sounded more like a reminiscence of a humdrum day at the office than a set of tenderly emotive miniatures.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/music/13-Thibaudet/13-Thibaudet--articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/music/13-Thibaudet/13-Thibaudet--articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/music/13-Thibaudet/13-Thibaudet--jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ