: 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Wai', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfb81624-42ff-5d96-8ce0-7471a3deb133', 'word_count': 1153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfb81624-42ff-5d96-8ce0-7471a3deb133'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: A poet and a lawyer duel in France in 1890.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/13/1890-a-poet-and-a-lawyer-duel/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: A poet and a lawyer duel in France in 1890.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLEROI — The Laguerre-Déroulède duel has been fought, and both principals are now languishing in jail here. They have had such an extraordinary hard time of it, trying to find a place to fight, that all Europe has been watching their movements. First, the French police dogged them, then the Belgian police tracked them about, next the Dutch officers objected to any gore being shed in Holland, and so the weary duellists wandered about in search of a nice quiet place, until they reached hospitable Charleroi. The ending of the combat was characteristic of the two men — one a poet and the other a cool-headed lawyer. It was all business on one side and all poetry on the other. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/14/blogs/14iht-oldnov14-poet/14iht-oldnov14-poet-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 839, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/14/blogs/14iht-oldnov14-poet/14iht-oldnov14-poet-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 839}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur