thy for her clients, she details her privileged childhood in Chicago; marriage into a wealthy East Coast family; and professional passion found later in life. As our reviewer, Alexandra Jacobs, put it: “She might be a bird in a gilded cage, but her view of the flowers outside is unobstructed.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T15:39:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joumana Khatib', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joumana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khatib', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/694a8bdd-471e-563b-8d46-a6ba07aea164', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/694a8bdd-471e-563b-8d46-a6ba07aea164'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUBMISSION, by Michel Houellebecq. Translated by Lorin Stein. (Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $25.) In Houellebecq’s morally complex novel, an alienated French professor and a France without faith or values yield to an Islamic government.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T15:39:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/afa838d1-5301-5728-b726-12961b0ecfdf', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afa838d1-5301-5728-b726-12961b0ecfdf'}, {'abstract': 'The star talks about working with her husband on “Secret in Their Eyes,” gender disparity in Hollywood and her coming movie with George Clooney.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/movies/julia-roberts-in-secret-in-their-eyes-turns-her-famous-smile-to-grief.html', 'snippet': 'The star talks about working with her husband on “Secret in Their Eyes,” gender disparity in Hollywood and her coming movie with George Clooney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Julia Roberts was meant to be a man, at least in her new movie, “Secret in Their Eyes.” Her character in this noirish murder thriller was written as a man who loses his wife. That’s how it was in the original Oscar-winning Argentine drama upon which the film is based. But in the American remake, written and directed by Billy Ray (the screenwriter of “Captain Phillips”) and co-starring Chiwetel Ejiofor and Nicole Kidman, it is Ms. Roberts who loses a family member, this time her daughter. Those script changes, conceived by Ms. Roberts, “had a seismic effect on the movie,” Mr. Ray said, giving audiences a new emotional entry point even as the character’s personality did not otherwise change.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15JULIAROBERTS1/15JULIAROBERTS1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15JULIAROBERTS1/15JULIAROBERTS1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15JULIAROBERTS1/15JULIAROBERTS1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15JULIAROBERTS1/15JULIAROBERTS1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15JULIAROBERTS1/15JULIAROBERTS1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15JULIAROBERTS1/15JULIAROBERTS1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/arts/15JULIAROBERTS1/15JULIAROBERTS1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Julia Roberts, in ‘Secret in Their Eyes,’ Turns Her Famous Smile to Grief', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Famous Smile Turns to Grief', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, Julia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Money Monster (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ray, Billy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Secret in Their Eyes (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T15:40:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/915a9f47-6386-5bcc-85b4-b7d471b4f3a8', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/915a9f47-6386-5bcc-85b4-b7d471b4f3a8'}, {'abstract': 'A renewed conversation about limiting smoking in multiple-unit dwellings could at least get things moving toward the right place.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/nyregion/reigniting-a-health-issue-smoking-in-new-york-public-housing.html', 'snippet': 'A renewed conversation about limiting smoking in multiple-unit dwellings could at least get things moving toward the right place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael R. Bloomberg’s initiative to end smoking in bars and restaurants, a ban that went into effect on a Sunday night in March 2003, was easily the greatest success of his mayoralty, one even his most impassioned detractors eventually came to applaud. We may never stop elegizing the meatpacking district’s Florent and the fiery, diminished downtown bohemia the restaurant represented, but few of us miss coughing into a slab of bavette, whether it was because we ourselves were smoking or because the guy next to us, looking like Michel Houellebecq, was lifting a match to his fifth American Spirit. When the smoking prohibition took effect, fatalists predicted the decline of eating out, the end of fun, the death of the city. Revelry persevered.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/nyregion/15BIGsub/15BIGdd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 314, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/nyregion/15BIGsub/15BIGdd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 314}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/nyregion/15BIGsub/15BIGdd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/nyregion/15BIGsub/15BIGdd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 885, 'width': 1692, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/nyregion/15BIGsub/15BIGsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/nyregion/15BIGsub/15BIGsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/nyregion/15BIGsub/15BIGsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Smoking Returns as a Hot Issue in New York Public Housing', 'kicker': 'Big City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Smoke and the City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public and Subsidized Housing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing and Urban Development Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing Authority (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], '