'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/14/sports/14fantasymatchup1/14fantasymatchup1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy Football Week 10: Rankings and Matchup Analysis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fantasy Sports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T16:08:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Sablich and Justin Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3416bc99-668b-5e45-8407-096f47c8a8d6', 'word_count': 3588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3416bc99-668b-5e45-8407-096f47c8a8d6'}, {'abstract': 'Donald Trump and Ben Carson are duking it out in the Republican debates — and on the hardcover nonfiction best-seller list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Donald Trump and Ben Carson are duking it out in the Republican debates — and on the hardcover nonfiction best-seller list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No Joy: The Republican debates aren’t the only place Donald Trump and Ben Carson are duking it out these days. They’re also competing on the hardcover nonfiction list, where Carson’s book “A More Perfect Union” (written with his wife, Candy Carson) is No.\u202f4 after five weeks and Trump’s book “Crippled America” makes its debut at No.\u202f5. Both men have been here before, of course, Carson with two previous best sellers and Trump with eight (most notably “Trump: The Art of the Deal,” written with Tony Schwartz, which spent 48 weeks on the hardcover nonfiction list in 1987 and 1988). So what do their books reveal about the candidates? Nothing we didn’t know already from watching them campaign. Carson is genial and bland in advancing his model of Christian conservatism; in his latest book, a paean to the Constitution, each chapter opens with a Bible verse. And Trump is blustery and vain and entertaining. Here he is discussing his book’s cover, which shows him with a stern Sam the Eagle frown: “Some readers may be wondering why the picture we used on the cover of this book is so angry and so mean looking. I had some beautiful pictures taken in which I had a big smile on my face. I looked happy, I looked content, I looked like a very nice person, which in theory I am. My family loved those pictures and wanted me to use one of them. The photographer did a great job. But I decided it wasn’t appropriate. In this book we’re talking about Crippled America — that’s a tough title. Unfortunately, there’s very little that’s nice about it. .\u2008.\u2008. So I wanted a picture where I wasn’t happy, a picture that reflected the anger and unhappiness that I feel, rather than joy. There’s nothing to be joyful about. Because we are not in a joyous situation right now.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22trump/22trump-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22trump/22trump-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22trump/22trump-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22trump/22trump-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22trump/22trump-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T16:08:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6574642f-86b1-5609-8d19-e3b8b074c1e2', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6574642f-86b1-5609-8d19-e3b8b074c1e2'}, {'abstract': 'In the wake of World War I, survivors explore new ways to connect to lost loved ones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/we-that-are-left-by-clare-clark.html', 'snippet': 'In the wake of World War I, survivors explore new ways to connect to lost loved ones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s an exchange halfway through Clare Clark’s fine new novel that gets to the heart of the book’s concerns. The speakers are Oscar Greenwood, studying physics at Cambridge just when Einstein’s theory of relativity is gaining widespread attention, and Phyllis Melville, a World War I nurse who, post-armistice, is intent on becoming an archaeologist. “Just because we can’t picture something,” Oscar tells her, “doesn’t mean it isn’t true.”\u2009“I thought scientists weren’t allowed to say things like that,” she observes. His reply? “These days they are.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15UPCHURCH/15UPCHURCH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 891, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15UPCHURCH/15UPCHURCH-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 891}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15UPCHURCH/15UPCHURCH-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 690, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15UPCHURCH/15UPCHURCH-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1800, 'width': 1212, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15UPCHURCH/15UPCHURCH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15UPCHURCH/15UPCHURCH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15UPCHURCH/15UPCHURCH-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘We That Are Left,’ by Clare Clark', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spirits of an Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Clare (1967- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'We That Are Left (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T16:10:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Upchurch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Upchurch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a5091f9-f55a-5efe-91e7-1cc1eb4233f1', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a5091f9-f55a-5efe-91e7-1cc1eb4233f1'}, {'abstract': 'Gloria Steinem reviews her peripatetic years as an activist and journalist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/gloria-steinems-my-life-on-the-road.html', 'snippet': 'Gloria Steinem reviews her peripatetic years as an activist and journalist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For many public intellectuals these days, speaking engagements pay the bills. But listening, according to the feminist icon Gloria Steinem, is even more important. Her new book, “My Life on the Road,” provides a lesson in how to stay relevant when your name is synonymous with a decades-old movement that has fallen in and out of popular favor: Keep moving. And keep asking questions.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15Friedman/1