e': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9d60901-5a79-559a-acf5-170a4aa12b17', 'word_count': 1319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9d60901-5a79-559a-acf5-170a4aa12b17'}, {'abstract': 'A novel interweaves two Antarctic expeditions, a century apart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/rebecca-hunts-everland.html', 'snippet': 'A novel interweaves two Antarctic expeditions, a century apart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is 1913, and a British expedition to Antarctica runs into disaster. Three members of the crew — Napps, a natural if overbearing leader; Millet-Bass, a physically imposing veteran of sea and ice; and Dinners, a young man inexperienced in everything to do with exploration life and the Antarctic — set out in a dinghy from the mother ship, Kismet, to explore the island they have named Everland in homage to their patron, Joseph Evelyn. But a storm arises as the men depart and when eventually they wash ashore their task is no longer exploration but survival. Echoes of Captain Scott’s disastrous Antarctic expedition ring through the story of the fictitious Kismet, but are never overplayed, and the Kismet’s venture slips plausibly among the other known sagas of this so-called Heroic Age of exploration.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Alexander/1115-BKS-Alexander-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 838, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Alexander/1115-BKS-Alexander-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 838}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Alexander/1115-BKS-Alexander-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 734, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Alexander/1115-BKS-Alexander-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1654, 'width': 1184, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Alexander/1115-BKS-Alexander-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Alexander/1115-BKS-Alexander-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Alexander/1115-BKS-Alexander-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rebecca Hunt’s ‘Everland’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out on the Ice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunt, Rebecca', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Everland (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T16:10:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Alexander', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alexander', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/489744d3-d930-5448-806b-5f5d2c96eb1d', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/489744d3-d930-5448-806b-5f5d2c96eb1d'}, {'abstract': 'A panoramic look at the experience of Germans from all walks of life during World War II. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/the-german-war-by-nicholas-stargardt.html', 'snippet': 'A panoramic look at the experience of Germans from all walks of life during World War II. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'World War II was extraordinary not only for the violence the Third Reich unleashed, but also for the way Hitler’s Germany went down to defeat. Kamikaze-style, suicidal defense will forever be associated with Japan. But it was Nazi Germany that actually immolated itself. While Hitler lived, there could be no negotiation. There would be no surrender, no repeat of the November 1918 armistice. The result was the devastation not only of much of Central Europe, but also of Germany itself. In the course of the war, 5.3\u202fmillion men would die fighting in Wehrmacht uniform. Close to a million German civilians were killed in air raids and the ethnic cleansing that followed the war. Germany’s casualties far exceeded those of all of Western Europe put together. And the violence escalated as the war went on. The largest number died in the last desperate months, the worst being January 1945, when the Wehrmacht lost 450,000 — more in a matter of weeks than America would suffer in all of its wars of the 20th century.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15subTOOZE/15subTOOZE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 735, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15subTOOZE/15subTOOZE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 735}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15subTOOZE/15subTOOZE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 836, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15subTOOZE/15subTOOZE-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1496, 'width': 1221, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15subTOOZE/15subTOOZE-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15subTOOZE/15subTOOZE-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/15subTOOZE/15subTOOZE-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The German War,’ by Nicholas Stargardt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Lives Untouched', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tooze, Adam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stargardt, Nicholas (1962- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The German War: A Nation Under Arms, 1939-1945 (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T16:10:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Tooze', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tooze', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a43abb7-7120-5660-8cdd-7709aa7e76d5', 'word_count': 1284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a43abb7-7120-5660-8cdd-7709aa7e76d5'}, {'abstract': 'Reich argues that capitalism should create broad prosperity, not just vast gains for the wealthy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/robert-reichs-saving-capitalism.html', 'snippet': 'Reich argues that capitalism should create broad prosperity, not just vast gains for the wealthy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To understand “Saving Capitalism,” Robert Reich’s sweeping treatise on inequality in America, you must accept a central premise: The free market is fundamentally a human construct and so to debate the appropriateness of government in shaping it is beside the point. Someone is always writing the rules of the market. Reich’s concern is that over the last three decades, the lead authors have been Wall Street, big corporations and the wealthy elite. His fear is that “we are lurching toward a capitalism so top-heavy it cannot be sustained.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Griswold/1115-BKS-Griswold-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 604, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Griswold/1115-BKS-Griswold-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 604}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Griswold/1115-BKS-Griswold-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1017, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Griswold/1115-BKS-Griswold-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1154, 'width': 1146, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Griswold/1115-BKS-Griswold-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Griswold/1115-BKS-Griswold-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Griswold/1115-BKS-Griswold-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Reich’s ‘Saving Capitalism’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unequal Opportunity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reich, Robert B', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capitalism (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Saving Capitalism (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T16:10:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Griswold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Griswold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/851fb2e0-c383-5477-86be-5ca469654be7', 'word_count': 593, 'uri': 'nyt://article/851fb2e0-c383-5477-86be-5ca469654be7'}, {'abstract': 'In Cornwell’s latest novel, the medical examiner Kay Scarpetta is worried about threats to her brilliant, prickly niece.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/patricia-cornwells-depraved-heart-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Cornwell’s latest novel, the medical examiner Kay Scarpetta is worried about threats to her brilliant, prickly niece.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Kay Scarpetta, who keeps us coming back to Patricia Cornwell’s sprawling crime novels, is one tough broad. As chief medical examiner for the state of Massachusetts, she has no trouble dealing with the gory sights and smells of dead bodies and violent crimes. “A select few of us come into this world not bothered by gruesomeness,” she says. “In fact we’re drawn to it, fascinated, intrigued.” What she can’t handle are threats to the person she loves best in the world, her brilliant, prickly niece, Lucy.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Crime/1115-BKS-Crime-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Crime/1115-BKS-Crime-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 769}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Crime/1115-BKS-Crime-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 799, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Crime/1115-BKS-Crime-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1987, 'width': 1550, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Crime/1115-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Crime/1115-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Crime/1115-BKS-Crime-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Patricia Cornwell’s ‘Depraved Heart,’ and More', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suitable for Framing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornwell, Patricia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kellerman, Faye', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kellerman, Jesse', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kellerman, Jonathan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Home by Nightfall (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Promise (Book)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Golem of Paris (Book)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Theory of Death (Book)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Depraved Heart (Book)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T16:10:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a086bcf-c750-519f-ac6f-b630ca05205e', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a086bcf-c750-519f-ac6f-b630ca05205e'}, {'abstract': 'Tallent’s stories show how easily love can alter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/elizabeth-tallents-mendocino-fire.html', 'snippet': 'Tallent’s stories show how easily love can alter.', 'lead_paragraph': '“How could narrators so prodigal in their empathy originate in the brain of that withholder?” So muses the unnamed woman who speaks to us in Elizabeth Tallent’s story “Narrator,” which comes near the end of her enchanting new collection, “Mendocino Fire.” That “withholder” is a famous writer, sprawled on the steps of the stairwell at a “welcome get-\xadtogether” for students in the creative writing program at Berkeley, and she has just watched him be flamboyantly rude to two young female admirers. Having thought highly of his novels, the new student has imagined the great man must have a “radiance” and “generosity of spirit” that are clearly not on offer. She feels betrayed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Martin/1115-BKS-Martin-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 1047, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Martin/1115-BKS-Martin-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1047}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/