/afd24d93-8991-5e19-ac97-971fd5fe0937', 'word_count': 1158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afd24d93-8991-5e19-ac97-971fd5fe0937'}, {'abstract': 'A young boy lands in the service of Pizarro in this novel of conquest, culture clash and shifting identity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/the-gold-eaters-by-ronald-wright.html', 'snippet': 'A young boy lands in the service of Pizarro in this novel of conquest, culture clash and shifting identity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The action of Ronald Wright’s fast-paced historical novel unfolds in the early 16th century in what is now Peru. His central character, Waman, is a callow boy from the coastal lowland town of Little River. At home, Waman speaks Tallan, an indigenous language that is, thanks to the spread of the Inca empire, giving way to Quechua, the tongue of the increasingly dominant Incas.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Wheeler/1115-BKS-Wheeler-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 353, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Wheeler/1115-BKS-Wheeler-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 353}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Wheeler/1115-BKS-Wheeler-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Wheeler/1115-BKS-Wheeler-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1205, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Wheeler/1115-BKS-Wheeler-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Wheeler/1115-BKS-Wheeler-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Wheeler/1115-BKS-Wheeler-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Gold Eaters,’ by Ronald Wright', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Among the Conquistadors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, Ronald', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Gold Eaters (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T16:10:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Wheeler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheeler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5cc543e-26b7-58de-ab7d-ec44016a251c', 'word_count': 562, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5cc543e-26b7-58de-ab7d-ec44016a251c'}, {'abstract': 'The many lives of a cross-dressing, swashbuckling opera star.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/books/review/goddess-by-kelly-gardiner.html', 'snippet': 'The many lives of a cross-dressing, swashbuckling opera star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The divine creature who plummets “from the painted clouds” to center stage in Kelly Gardiner’s gender-bending picaresque, “Goddess,” is based on an actual historical character. Julie d’Aubigny, a.k.a. Mademoiselle de Maupin, a.k.a. La Travesti, grew up in Versailles on the periphery of Louis XIV’s court, where her father served as secretary to the Sun King’s master of horse. Papa d’Aubigny taught the court pages — and his daughter, who from childhood on dressed “as a chevalier who happened to be born female” — to fence; and Julie became an expert swordsman and equestrian, a seductress of both women and men, a tavern brawler and itinerant singer, and improbably — but what is not improbable in this narrative? — an operatic superstar at the Académie Royale de Musique in Paris. She died in a convent in 1707 at the age of 33.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Kline/1115-BKS-Kline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 869, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Kline/1115-BKS-Kline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 869}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Kline/1115-BKS-Kline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 708, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Kline/1115-BKS-Kline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1167, 'width': 806, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/15/books/review/1115-BKS-Kline/1115-BKS-Kline-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'im