 Hollande called an unprecedented terrorist attack on France. His government announced sharply increased border controls and heightened police powers as it mobilized the military in a national emergency.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Attacks Kill More Than 100, Police Say; Border Controls Tightened', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Series of Shootings and Blasts, Apparently Coordinated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Attacks (November 2015)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hollande, Francois', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bataclan (Paris, France)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stade de France (Paris, France)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T21:50:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nossiter and Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossiter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23218c6e-34ac-5faa-a2c5-b2ca049b32e0', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23218c6e-34ac-5faa-a2c5-b2ca049b32e0'}, {'abstract': 'For Mr. Calise, who has been paralyzed from the waist down since 1994, the effort to make New York City’s public spaces more accessible never stops.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/15/nyregion/how-victor-calise-disabilities-commissioner-spends-his-sundays.html', 'snippet': 'For Mr. Calise, who has been paralyzed from the waist down since 1994, the effort to make New York City’s public spaces more accessible never stops.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Mayor Bill de Blasio reappointed Victor Calise this year as the commissioner for the Mayor’s Office for People With Disabilities, he remarked on “Victor’s relentless pursuit to make New York City the most accessible place in the world.” Mr. Calise, 43, was raised in Ozone Park, Queens, and has been using a wheelchair since a mountain biking accident in 1994 that left him paralyzed from the waist down. He competed on the United States sled hockey team at the 1998 Paralympics in Japan. Later, he worked with the parks department to help bring the city’s parks, beaches and recreational facilities into compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act. He lives on the Upper West Side with his wife, Susan, and their daughters, Sophia, 13, and Lola, 10. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Victor Calise, Disabilities Commissioner, Spends His Sundays', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Soccer and Sauce, All on Wheels  ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Correal, Annie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-13T21:56:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Correal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Correal', 'qualifier': None, 'title':