, 'pub_date': '2015-11-16T23:51:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gately and Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gately', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1afeaabb-9f9d-5456-ad14-38962f681cb9', 'word_count': 893, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1afeaabb-9f9d-5456-ad14-38962f681cb9'}, {'abstract': 'Unlike after the Charlie Hebdo attacks in January, calls for solidarity with Muslims have been rare while even the left-wing government considers harsher security measures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/17/world/europe/after-paris-attacks-a-darker-mood-toward-islam-emerges-in-france.html', 'snippet': 'Unlike after the Charlie Hebdo attacks in January, calls for solidarity with Muslims have been rare while even the left-wing government considers harsher security measures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — November is not January. That thought has been filtering through the statements of most French politicians and the news media, and most people seem to understand.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Paris Attacks, a Darker Mood Toward Islam Emerges in France', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Distrust, Even Fear, as Secular France Cools on Muslims', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Attacks (November 2015)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'v