15/11/17/fashion/17LAAR-slide-QS5G/17LAAR-slide-QS5G-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How They Dress Up for the Non-Office', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-17T10:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By JOHN ORTVED', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ORTVED', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/813aa6a1-7620-5dbc-aeb2-492966bfd418', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/813aa6a1-7620-5dbc-aeb2-492966bfd418'}, {'abstract': 'Lafayette was hailed as a hero on his 1820s American tour.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/books/review/sarah-vowells-lafayette-in-the-somewhat-united-states.html', 'snippet': 'Lafayette was hailed as a hero on his 1820s American tour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The full name was Marie-Joseph-Paul-Yves-Roch-Gilbert du Motier, which was quite a mouthful for Yankee sensibilities, but the Marquis de Lafayette became one of the first American celebrities anyway. (The new nation had a sweet tooth for the flattery of Frenchmen. That’s also how Tocqueville managed to freeload his way across the Northwest Territories.) Lafayette came over the Atlantic to fight in the American Revolution when he was 19. He went home to France and tried to be a centrist politician during that country’s upheaval. This was rather like aspiring to be a pot roast in a den of wolverines. But he survived the Revolution and the Terror, turned down a job in Napoleon’s government, and in 1824, he returned to the United States at the invitation of James Monroe. He visited all 24 of the states at the time. The tour took more than a year, and Lafayette was cheered at every stop. He survived the sinking of his steamboat in the Ohio River, and lived to help lay the cornerstone of the Bunker Hill Monument in Charlestown, Mass. He also survived an oration by Daniel Webster on that same occasion.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22PIERCE/22PIERCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 793, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22PIERCE/22PIERCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 793}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22PIERCE/22PIERCE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 775, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22PIERCE/22PIERCE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1550, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22PIERCE/22PIERCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22PIERCE/22PIERCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22PIERCE/22PIERCE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sarah Vowell’s ‘Lafayette in the Somewhat United States’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grand Marquis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vowell, Sarah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lafayette, Marquis de', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Lafayette in the Somewhat United States (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-17T10:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles P. Pierce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Pierce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/36397dcd-126f-5fe3-8b28-8d8b2a7d2037', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36397dcd-126f-5fe3-8b28-8d8b2a7d2037'}, {'abstract': 'One book sees a problem with applied economics; another sees a perversion of the Chicago school’s message.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/books/review/chicagonomics-and-economics-rules.html', 'snippet': 'One book sees a problem with applied economics; another sees a perversion of the Chicago school’s message.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He believed that government had a crucial role to play in a well-functioning economy. It should finance and run good schools, as well as build roads, bridges and parks, he argued. It should tax alcohol, sugar and tobacco, all of which impose costs on society. It should regulate businesses to protect workers. And it should tax the rich — who suffer from “indolence and vanity” — to help the poor.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non