'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-17T15:44:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c914b060-9621-5c7b-a085-a1fcafd19826', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c914b060-9621-5c7b-a085-a1fcafd19826'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Italy strikes at enemies in Greece, Crete and Egypt in 1940.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/17/1940-italians-bomb-foes/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Italy strikes at enemies in Greece, Crete and Egypt in 1940.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROME — New triumphs for Italy’s aviators were reported today [Nov. 17] by the army high command, which said that in Greece, ‘‘where attacks and counter-attacks were especially fierce in the sector of the 9th army,’’ Italian aviation ‘‘bombed roads, railways, enemy positions and artillery posts, causing fires and explosions.’’ The port of Alexandria and the harbor of Suda, in Crete, have again been bombed, it was said, as well as British bases in Egypt. In Egypt, it was announced, ‘‘our aviation has bombed bases at El Daba and Maaten Bagush, equipment at Mersa Matruh and the railroad from there to Bir Amasin.’’ — New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, November 18, 1940', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1940: Italians Bomb Foes', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Crete (G