3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amherst, Jeffery (1717-97)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMHERST COLLEGE', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T01:12:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anemona Hartocollis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anemona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartocollis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a95bdd16-90f4-5e55-9dc1-94ef868d6348', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a95bdd16-90f4-5e55-9dc1-94ef868d6348'}, {'abstract': 'Indiscriminate bulk data sweeps have not been useful, yet intelligence officials complain that restrictions impede their efforts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/18/opinion/mass-surveillance-isnt-the-answer-to-fighting-terrorism.html', 'snippet': 'Indiscriminate bulk data sweeps have not been useful, yet intelligence officials complain that restrictions impede their efforts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a wretched yet predictable ritual after each new terrorist attack: Certain politicians and government officials waste no time exploiting the tragedy for their own ends. The remarks on Monday by John Brennan, the director of the Central Intelligence Agency, took that to a new and disgraceful low.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/opinion/18wed2web/18wed2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/18/opinion/18wed2web/18wed2web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/opinion/18wed2web/18wed2web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/opinion/18wed2web/18wed2web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1322, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/opinion/18wed2web/18wed2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/18/opinion/18wed2web/18wed2web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/opinion/18wed2web/18wed2web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mass Surveillance Isn’t the Answer to Fighting Terrorism', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mass Surveillance Isn’t the Answer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brennan, John O', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Attacks (November 2015)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T01:22:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d4463d2-f584-55c8-8605-888bddda68d6', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d4463d2-f584-55c8-8605-888bddda68d6'}, {'abstract': 'The police said that Jamar Clark was an assault suspect, and that he was shot after interfering with paramedics trying to treat his victim and fighting with two officers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/18/us/minnesota-man-shot-by-police-dies.html', 'snippet': 'The police said that Jamar Clark was an assault suspect, and that he was shot after interfering with paramedics trying to treat his victim and fighting with two officers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An unarmed black man shot Sunday by the Minneapolis police died on Monday evening, state investigators said. The police said that the man, Jamar Clark, 24, was an assault suspect, and that he had been shot after interfering with paramedics trying to treat his victim and fighting with two officers. The police disputed witness accounts that said Mr. Clark was handcuffed when he was shot. Though no police camera footage captured the episode, other cameras showed parts of the encounter, said Superintendent Drew Evans of the Minnesota Bureau of Criminal Apprehension, which is investigating the case. Protesters have demanded the release of video evidence and the names of the officers. Mayor Betsy Hodges has requested a federal civil rights investigation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Minnesota: Man Shot by Police Dies', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Minnesota: Man Shot By Police Dies ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Minneapolis (Minn)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T01:29:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By MITCH SMITH', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mitch', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SMITH', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4a89bb0-b2fa-5475-92a7-4f705cc51511', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4a89bb0-b2fa-5475-92a7-4f705cc51511'}, {'abstract': 'Syphilis, gonorrhea and chlamydia are rising at alarming rates, according to figures released by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/18/health/spike-seen-in-reported-cases-of-sexually-transmitted-diseases.html', 'snippet': 'Syphilis, gonorrhea and chlamydia are rising at alarming rates, according to figures released by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Syphilis, gonorrhea and chlamydia are rising at alarming rates, according to figures released Tuesday by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Syphilis infections reported by doctors rose 15 percent from 2013 to 2014; cases of gonorrhea increased 5 percent, and chlamydia 3 percent. The agency called the diseases “a substantial health challenge.” Syphilis cases have more than tripled since 2000, and 91 percent are in men — mostly gay and bisexual men. Dr. Gail Bolan, the agency’s director of prevention of sexually transmitted disease, said there was “no single answer” for the increase, but noted several factors. State and local spending on prevention and treatment has shrunk as screening of gay men has picked up. Many gay men start antiretroviral drugs as soon as they are infected with H.I.V., and others take Truvada to prevent infection; there have been reports that condom use has dropped in this population as a result. Although those drugs prevent H.I.V. infection and transmission, they do not prevent the spread of syphilis, gonorrhea, chlamydia, human papilloma virus, herpes or trichomoniasis. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spike Seen in Reported Cases of Sexually Transmitted Diseases', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spike Seen in Reported Cases of Sexually Transmitted Diseases', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNeil, Donald G Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexually Transmitted Diseases', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chlamydia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Syphilis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gonorrhea', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T01:34:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/746936ba-daad-5af2-bb96-ca8a8eb492f1', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/746936ba-daad-5af2-bb96-ca8a8eb492f1'}, {'abstract': 'Once billed as a rising Republican star, Gov. Bobby Jindal of Louisiana had his 2016 presidential bid fail to make it out of 2015, plagued by a flat message and flagging poll numbers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2015/11/17/timeline-of-bobby-jindals-presidential-campaign/', 'snippet': 'Once billed as a rising Republican star, Gov. Bobby Jindal of Louisiana had his 2016 presidential bid fail to make it out of 2015, plagued by a flat message and flagging poll numbers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once billed as a rising star in the Republican Party, Gov. Bobby Jindal of Louisiana had his 2016 presidential bid fail to make it out of 2015 as a flat message and flagging poll numbers plagued his campaign. Mr. Jindal remained fiery on stump until the end, but his conservative message did not resonate in this cycle’s crowded field. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/us/17firstdraft-timeline/17firstdraft-timeline-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/18/us/17firstdraft-timeline/17firstdraft-timeline-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/us/17firstdraft-timeline/17firstdraft-timeline-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/us/17firstdraft-timeline/17firstdraft-timeline-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/18/us/17firstdraft-timeline/17firstdraft-timeline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/18/us/17firstdraft-timeline/17firstdraft-timeline-thumbStandard.jpg', '