nk': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T15:41:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kimmelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kimmelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18165f31-788e-5de7-b1f8-5d9d9f533dc3', 'word_count': 940, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18165f31-788e-5de7-b1f8-5d9d9f533dc3'}, {'abstract': 'To a critic who has seen perhaps 50 versions, this ballet is both familiar and fascinating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/arts/dance/the-nutcracker-and-its-many-mysteries.html', 'snippet': 'To a critic who has seen perhaps 50 versions, this ballet is both familiar and fascinating.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Nutcracker,” though such a familiar fixture in the calendar, is among the most layered of all works of art. Yes, it contains kitsch and cuteness and adorability. Yes, it’s surrounded by merchandising. Even so, this ballet is larger, stranger, more moving than all of that — and more unknowable. There are mysteries at its heart that don’t vanish and wonders that don’t diminish.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22NUTCRACKER1/22NUTCRACKER1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22NUTCRACKER1/22NUTCRACKER1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22NUTCRACKER1/22NUTCRACKER1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22NUTCRACKER1/22NUTCRACKER1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1299, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22NUTCRACKER1/22NUTCRACKER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22NUTCRACKER1/22NUTCRACKER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22NUTCRACKER1/22NUTCRACKER1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Nutcracker’ and Its Many Mysteries', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Many Mysteries of ‘The Nutcracker’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Nutcracker (Ballet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tchaikovsky, Peter Ilyich', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Balanchine, George', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Mark', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T15:55:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8438d58-262c-5156-877f-b60f11b8ef92', 'word_count': 1302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8438d58-262c-5156-877f-b60f11b8ef92'}, {'abstract': 'The musicians, who collaborated on an album that was released in August, are briefly touring clubs together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/arts/music/miley-cyrus-and-the-flaming-lips-hit-the-clubs.html', 'snippet': 'The musicians, who collaborated on an album that was released in August, are briefly touring clubs together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was improbable enough that Miley Cyrus would collaborate with the Flaming Lips, the long-running Oklahoma band steeped in psychedelia, despite a shared interest in altered mental states. In August, they suddenly released a free online stream of the album “Miley Cyrus & Her Dead Petz,” which also had some contributions from the producer Mike WiLL Made-It. The album was an outlet for jokey thoughts on drugs and sex, for studio high jinks and for lyrics flaunting cuss words, but it also had a handful of full-fledged songs and glimmers of thoughtfulness. Now, even more improbably, Ms. Cyrus and the Flaming Lips are briefly touring clubs together. Dan Deacon, opening the show, is a synthesizer-wielding composer who stokes enthusiastic audience participation. (8 p.m. on Saturday at Terminal 5; 610 West 56th Street, 212-260-4700, $69.69.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 463, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 463}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 791, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1581, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22GUIDE3/22GUIDE3-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Miley Cyrus and the Flaming Lips Hit the Clubs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop; Hitting the Clubs With Miley Cyrus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cyrus, Miley', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Flaming Lips (Music Group)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Terminal 5 (Manhattan, NY, Music Venue)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T16:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf00ce09-59f2-5483-9e2d-01b9385a31fb', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf00ce09-59f2-5483-9e2d-01b9385a31fb'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: A teenager opens fire on a National Airlines plane in 1965.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/18/1965-hijacker-shoots-up-plane/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: A teenager opens fire on a National Airlines plane in 1965.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW ORLEANS — A frail teen-ager pulled out two pistols aboard a jetliner last night [Nov. 17] and nearly shot Christopher Kraft, Gemini space mission flight director, in the face. The youth was Thomas Robinson, 16, of Brownsville, Texas. He fired six shots aboard a National Airlines DC-8 jetliner before a passenger subdued him. No one was injured. Mr. Kraft, with a score of other top U.S. space officials traveling to Cape Kennedy for a flight readiness review for the next Gemini mission, was threatened by the frail-looking pistol-waving youth who said he was trying to hijack the plane and take it to Cuba.\xa0— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, November 19, 1965', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/19/blogs/19iht-oldnov19-kraft/19iht-oldnov19-kraft-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 471, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/19/blogs/19iht-oldnov19-kraft/19iht-oldnov19-kraft-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 471}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/19/blogs/19iht-oldnov19-kraft/19iht-oldnov19-kraft-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/19/blogs/19iht-oldnov19-kraft/19iht-oldnov19-kraft-s