stname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59b9b1e3-87c7-5847-8cc9-148ba14c1b9f', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59b9b1e3-87c7-5847-8cc9-148ba14c1b9f'}, {'abstract': 'in the program “The Other Side of Dreams,” films from the director are paired with movies by other directors that offer similar themes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/movies/todd-hayness-flash-and-finesse-at-the-film-society-of-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': 'in the program “The Other Side of Dreams,” films from the director are paired with movies by other directors that offer similar themes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Other Side of Dreams,” the Film Society of Lincoln Center’s lovingly curated retrospective of the films of Todd Haynes, selections are paired with films chosen by Mr. Haynes that influenced them. His suburban melodrama, “Far From Heaven,” is coupled with Max Ophuls’s “The Reckless Moment,” which also deals with a scandalous family secret. His HBO mini-series, “Mildred Pierce,” is connected to “Klute,” in which Jane Fonda’s Oscar-winning role as a hardened prostitute is markedly similar to Kate Winslet’s devoted, hard-driving mother in “Pierce.” The flamboyant David Bowie-like rocker (Jonathan Rhys Meyers) in “Velvet Goldmine,” set in London at the height of the glam-rock era, is not unlike the androgynous rock star played by Mick Jagger in Nicolas Roeg and Donald Cammell’s cult classic “Performance.” Mr. Haynes’s AIDS-era parable, “Safe,” is paired with Douglas Sirk’s final Hollywood film, “Imitation of Life,” a critique of racial and class divisions in America disguised as a sudsy weeper. (Through Nov. 29; filmlinc.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':