, 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T18:18:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1814c30-3404-5588-98a9-a099dd127e6c', 'word_count': 804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1814c30-3404-5588-98a9-a099dd127e6c'}, {'abstract': 'Misty Copeland, a principal dancer with American Ballet Theater, is expected to release her third book, “Ballerina Body,” in early 2017.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/18/misty-copeland-to-write-book-on-health-and-fitness/', 'snippet': 'Misty Copeland, a principal dancer with American Ballet Theater, is expected to release her third book, “Ballerina Body,” in early 2017.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Misty Copeland’s big year isn’t over yet. After making history by becoming American Ballet Theater’s first black female principal dancer, performing on Broadway to favorable reviews and starring in a documentary about her life, she will now write her third book.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Misty Copeland to Write Book on Health and Fitness', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Misty Copeland Writing a Fitness Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Copeland, Misty', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ratmansky, Alexei', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hachette Book Group', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Under Armour Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-18T18:30:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Barone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46ed55e2-faf3-5111-a076-c9d401394d88', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46ed55e2-faf3-5111-a076-c9d401394d88'}, {'abstract': 'There aren’t many gender differences in meanness. What’s different is how girls and boys respond.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/18/girls-arent-meaner-than-boys-it-only-looks-that-way/', 'snippet': 'There aren’t many gender differences in meanness. What’s different is how girls and boys respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Girls can be mean. Really mean. Yet despite their widespread reputation for cruelty, we have little evidence that girls are meaner than boys. It’s no surprise to learn that boys are more likely than girls to use physical aggression, but we also know that boys surpass girls when it comes to attacking peers verbally and engaging in cyberbullying. Some research suggests that girls pick up the slack by engaging in more relational aggression: being emotionally manipulative, spreading rumors, excluding peers. But other studies find that boys can outpace, or at least match, girls in the use of indirect forms of interpersonal treachery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/27/arts/27MEAN6_SPAN/27MEAN6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/04/27/arts