of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a948fd1-71df-5697-9e87-2f6e5ba06aee', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a948fd1-71df-5697-9e87-2f6e5ba06aee'}, {'abstract': 'Deniz Gamze Ergüven tells the story of what it is to be a girl and a woman in Turkey in France’s foreign-language Oscar submission.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/movies/with-mustang-a-director-breaks-free-of-cultural-confines.html', 'snippet': 'Deniz Gamze Ergüven tells the story of what it is to be a girl and a woman in Turkey in France’s foreign-language Oscar submission.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — For a film about death, “Mustang” is incredibly alive. It tells the story of five orphaned sisters in rural Turkey whose boundless lust for life runs up against the confines of a conservative Muslim society, leading some to suicide. A first feature by Deniz Gamze Ergüven — directed, written by and starring young women — it was a hit when it played the Directors’ Fortnight for independent films at Cannes and is France’s foreign-language Oscar submission.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22MUSTANG1/22MUSTANG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22MUSTANG1/22MUSTANG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22MUSTANG1/22MUSTANG1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/arts/22MUSTANG1/22MUSTANG1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': 