Viktor Ullmann are both lunatic satirical responses to the carnage of World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Juilliard Opera folks could not have known, when they programmed their current double bill, how appropriate it would be at a time of escalating international terrorism. Francis Poulenc’s “Les Mamelles de Tiresias” (“The Breasts of Tiresias,” 1947) and Viktor Ullmann’s “Der Kaiser von Atlantis, Oder der Tod Dankt Ab” (“The Emperor of Atlantis, or Death Abdicates,” 1943), though contrasting in style and mood, are both lunatic satirical responses to the carnage of World War II.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20JUILLARDOPERA1/20JUILLARDOPERA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20JUILLARDOPERA1/20JUILLARDOPERA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20JUILLARDOPERA1/20JUILLARDOPERA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20JUILLARDOPERA1/20JUILLARDOPERA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 837, 'width': 1307, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20JUILLARDOPERA1/20JUILLARDOPERA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20JUILLARDOPERA1/20JUILLARDOPERA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20JUILLARDOPERA1/20JUILLARDOPERA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Juilliard Opera’s Timely Double Bill', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Responses to Carnage From Another Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Juilliard Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poulenc, Francis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ullmann, Viktor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Der Kaiser von Atlantis, Oder der Tod Dankt Ab (Opera)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Les Mamelles de Tiresias (Opera)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-19T21:40:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/724b27d5-8100-513d-bf28-93d6d74d8418', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/724b27d5-8100-513d-bf28-93d6d74d8418'}, {'abstract': 'The attacks of Nov. 13 would not have happened if the government hadn’t let down its guard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/21/opinion/how-frances-leaders-failed-its-people.html', 'snippet': 'The attacks of Nov. 13 would not have happened if the government hadn’t let down its guard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paris — IN the aftermath of the January attacks in Paris, I spent two days transfixed watching the news. In the aftermath of the Nov. 13 attacks, I hardly turned on the television; I just called the people I knew (no small number) who lived in the neighborhoods that were hit. You get used to terrorist attacks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/opinion/20michel/20michel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/20/opinion/20michel/20michel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/opinion/20michel/20michel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/opinion/20michel/20michel-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1371, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/opinion/20michel/20michel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/20/opinion/20michel/20michel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/opinion/20michel/20michel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michel Houellebecq: How France’s Leaders Failed Its People', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France’s Leaders Failed It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Attacks (November 2015)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Houellebecq, Michel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-19T21:49:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michel Houellebecq', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Houellebecq', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/146f1c6b-adf6-5bf3-b5f7-ebf5e6199ea7', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/146f1c6b-adf6-5bf3-b5f7-ebf5e6199ea7'}, {'abstract': 'Solo exhibitions in the city by three women explore the medium in very different ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/19/t-magazine/los-angeles-female-video-artists.html', 'snippet': 'Solo exhibitions in the city by three women explore the medium in very different ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Video-based art is having a moment among women in Los Angeles. Three female artists with solo exhibitions on view there this fall aim to shift perceptions of the medium’s potential, using it to capture everything from Op Art-like abstractions to provocative depictions of animal life. Diana Thater, whose career-spanning show opens next week at the Los Angeles County Museum of Art, posits that the appeal of video for women might come from the medium’s relative newness — there are no old masters glowering from the textbook pages. “Women could enter into it without this art-historical legacy,” Thater says. “There is no male-dominated history, so there’s more freedom.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/19/t-magazine/19tmag-video-2/19tmag-v