aragraph': 'A Hollywood feature like “Sicario” might strive to capture the merciless grip drug cartels have on Mexico, but Bernardo Ruiz’s visceral documentary “Kingdom of Shadows” gives you a frontline glimpse. Three perspectives predominate: those of Sister Consuelo Morales, a Roman Catholic nun seeking answers for families of the “disappeared” (civilians who have vanished because of drug gangs or police corruption); Don Henry Ford Jr., a Texas rancher who served time for smuggling marijuana from Mexico; and the Homeland Security officer Oscar Hagelsieb, a former undercover operative for the Border Patrol.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20KINGDOM1/20KINGDOM1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20KINGDOM1/20KINGDOM1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 510}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20KINGDOM1/20KINGDOM1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 858, 'width': 1010, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20KINGDOM1/20KINGDOM1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 858, 'width': 1010, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20KINGDOM1/20KINGDOM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/20/arts/20KINGDOM1/20KINGDOM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None,