ub_date': '2015-11-20T02:08:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecb98c0c-f0e7-597a-befd-30b0f61a1c81', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecb98c0c-f0e7-597a-befd-30b0f61a1c81'}, {'abstract': 'A federal judge in Colorado on Thursday upheld the 2008 law that legalized the Bush administration’s warrantless surveillance program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/20/us/judge-upholds-2008-surveillance-law.html', 'snippet': 'A federal judge in Colorado on Thursday upheld the 2008 law that legalized the Bush administration’s warrantless surveillance program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge in Colorado on Thursday upheld the 2008 law that legalized the Bush administration’s warrantless surveillance program, turning back a constitutional challenge by the first criminal defendant notified by the government that he faced evidence derived from such wiretapping. The 2008 law permits the government to conduct surveillance on domestic soil without warrants so long as its target is a noncitizen abroad, even when that foreign target is communicating with an American. The defendant, Jamshid Muhtorov, was charged in Colorado in January 2012 with providing material support to the Islamic Jihad Union, a terrorist organization based in Uzbekistan. He was notified that he had been monitored under the 2008 law after a policy shift at the Justice Department. In the first ruling on the issue, a judge overseeing another terrorism-related case in Oregon upheld the wiretapping law in June 2014. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judge Upholds 2008 Surveillance Law ', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Judge Upholds 2008 Surveillance Law ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywo