e, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T16:13:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/605fb7fa-cc44-55c4-afed-a5d108e62f9c', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/605fb7fa-cc44-55c4-afed-a5d108e62f9c'}, {'abstract': 'Mitch Albom’s latest heartwarming and slightly gooey best seller is about a supernaturally gifted guitarist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Mitch Albom’s latest heartwarming and slightly gooey best seller is about a supernaturally gifted guitarist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beautiful Music: Almost six years ago, when Inside the List asked a handful of authors to declare their literary New Year’s resolutions, Mitch Albom pledged “to read more books about hope and less about angst.” It was no surprise, frankly. Can anybody picture Albom — whose heartwarming and slightly gooey best sellers include “Tuesdays With Morrie” and “The Five People You Meet in Heaven” — settling in for the night with, say, a dyspeptic Thomas Bernhard novel? Now Albom’s back with “The Magic Strings of Frankie Presto,” about a supernaturally gifted guitarist who influences musicians as diverse as Hank Williams and Wynton Marsalis and Kiss; it’s new on the hardcover fiction list at No.\u202f4. A musician himself, Albom plays keyboards with the Rock Bottom Remainders, where his bandmates include Amy Tan on vocals and Stephen King on rhythm guitar, among assorted other best-selling authors. (“We mostly listen to Dave Barry telling booger jokes,” Albom has said.) “The theme I was trying to build on for this book was that everybody joins a band in life,” he told The Philadelphia Daily News this month. “Everyone joins a band. We may not think we do, but we do. Somebody plays the lead. Somebody is the rhythm. Somebody holds down the beat. Somebody gets to solo and go all over the place, while the other person gets stuck playing the chords. That’s pretty much how it is in a family. That’s how it is in the Army. That’s how it is in a workplace. Everybody has a role that they play.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/books/review/20albom/20albom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/books/review/20albom/20albom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/books/review/20albom/20albom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/20/books/review/20albom/20albom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/books/review/20albom/20albom-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T16:19:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_