', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/multimedia/clark-squash-salad/clark-squash-salad-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/20/multimedia/clark-squash-salad/clark-squash-salad-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/20/multimedia/clark-squash-salad/clark-squash-salad-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Roasted Squash Salad', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Squash (Vegetable)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salads', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T17:06:04+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By ALEC COHEN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alec', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'COHEN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c211af42-ec7b-5e9a-a787-0d1df57f6138', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c211af42-ec7b-5e9a-a787-0d1df57f6138'}, {'abstract': 'Roasted vegetables add the necessary warming bulk, and their browned edges also bring depth to the bowl’s fresh greenery. (Article plus video.)', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/25/dining/delicata-squash-salad-recipe-video.html', 'snippet': 'Roasted vegetables add the necessary warming bulk, and their browned edges also bring depth to the bowl’s fresh greenery. (Article plus video.)', 'lead_paragraph': 'Summer salads have it easy. With a bounty of greens in season for the taking, it’s easy for them to be fresh, bright and pretty.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Squash Gives This Winter Salad a Satisfying Twist', 'kicker': 'A Good Appetite', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Squash Takes Winter Salad for a Satisfying Ride', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Melissa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salads', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Squash (Vegetable)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T17:06:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e944b8fe-2980-5baa-a202-9f89b40df977', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e944b8fe-2980-5baa-a202-9f89b40df977'}, {'abstract': 'A survey of the Pacific includes chapters on coral reefs, thermonuclear bombs and cyclones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/books/review/simon-winchesters-pacific.html', 'snippet': 'A survey of the Pacific includes chapters on coral reefs, thermonuclear bombs and cyclones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the decades, the British-born Simon Winchester has emerged as one of those increasingly rare geographic generalists, migrating from region to region, sharing with his readers an almost tactile sense of the earth, its history and its peoples. His narratives exist within that depressingly vast space between the hairsplitting nuances and abstractions of academia and the vulgar simplicities of cable news pundits. A highbrow out to educate middlebrows, Winchester writes books like someone telling a good yarn around the fireplace, whether about volcanoes, Indian slums or the 19th-century American West. He is an outdoorsman who knows about such things as geology and ocean sailing, which invigorates his prose in the same way as that of another journalistic travel writer, William Langewiesche, who flies planes and also sails. And he certainly has the knack for comparison. When Winchester writes that Hawaii, deep down, “in its perceived cultural essence,” still evokes what “was felt by Gauguin during his time in the Marquesas,” it is reminiscent of the British travel writer Jan Morris describing in 1960 how the smells and landscape of the Lido in Venice ignite thoughts of Carthage and Marrakesh.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22KAPLAN/KAPLAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 315, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22KAPLAN/KAPLAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 315}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22KAPLAN/KAPLAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 537, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22KAPLAN/KAPLAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1075, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22KAPLAN/KAPLAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22KAPLAN/KAPLAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22KAPLAN/KAPLAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Simon Winchester’s ‘Pacific’', 'kicker': 'Audiobooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ocean of Wonders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winchester, Simon (1944- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audiobooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Pacific: Silicon Chips and Surfboards, Coral Reefs and Atom Bombs, Brutal Dictators, Fading Empires, and the Coming Collision of the World's Superpowers (Book)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T17:09:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert D. Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f4f87ea-c9d1-51d2-a367-0f59b2e3d825', 'word_count': 1154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f4f87ea-c9d1-51d2-a367-0f59b2e3d825'}, {'abstract': 'At an authentically cozy French bistro on Grand Street, a trio of Parisians mourned, talked, worried, remembered and toasted one another.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/nyregion/paris-attack-parisians-in-new-york-reflect-at-parigot-french-restaurant-in-soho.html', 'snippet': 'At an authentically cozy French bistro on Grand Street, a trio of Parisians mourned, talked, worried, remembered and toasted one another.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who knows if it’s their own sensuality or the sensuous nature of their city, but some Parisians can live abroad for decades and still describe, as though they had smelled it only yesterday, that precise, blended odor of baking baguette and rainy sidewalk that hovers outside their favorite bakery on the Rue Lamarck. One imagines that this sensorial attachment to Paris was never stronger than in recent days, after the city suffered terrorist attacks that killed more than 120 people. At Parigot, an authentically cozy French bistro on Grand Street in SoHo, a trio of Parisians mourned, talked, worried, remembered and toasted one another, both missing their city and at the same time perhaps feeling glad that they were far away.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22TABLE1/22TABLE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22TABLE1/22TABLE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22TABLE1/22TABLE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22TABLE1/22TABLE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1195, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22TABLE1/22TABLE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22TABLE1/22TABLE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22TABLE1/22TABLE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Parigot in SoHo, Parisians Reflect on Terror Attacks', 'kicker': 'At the Table', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In SoHo, Reflections on Paris  ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feuer, Alan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Attacks (November 2015)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SoHo (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parigot (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T17:10:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Feuer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feuer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddd71ca3-8d01-585d-b539-23b4bbe6ab16', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddd71ca3-8d01-585d-b539-23b4bbe6ab16'}, {'abstract': 'The skull of Winnipeg, the bear that inspired “Winnie-the-Pooh,” has gone on display in London.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/20/winnie-the-poohs-skull-relic-of-inspirational-bear-goes-on-display/', 'snippet': 'The skull of Winnipeg, the bear that inspired “Winnie-the-Pooh,” has gone on display in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The skull of Winnipeg, the bear who inspired “Winnie-the-Pooh,” has gone on display for the first time at the Hunterian Museum in London.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Winnie-the-Pooh’s Skull: Relic of Inspirational Bear Goes on Display', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Winnie-the-Pooh (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Milne, A A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hunterian Museum', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'London Zoo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal College of Surgeons', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T17:11:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher D. Shea', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b01b0286-df31-5934-957b-f2f752328b29', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b01b0286-df31-5934-957b-f2f752328b29'}, {'abstract': 'Malkovich reads an offbeat 1973 novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/books/review/kurt-vonneguts-breakfast-of-champions-read-by-john-malkovich.html', 'snippet': 'Malkovich reads an offbeat 1973 novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Forgive me for starting with an obvious point, but it’s one I hadn’t really considered before I was asked to review an audiobook, or as they used to be called, “books on CD,” and before that, “books on tape,” and before that, “readings,” and before that, “storytelling.” The point is this: All reading is a form of reading aloud, even when the reading is done in silence. The very act of transferring the words of another into the brain activates the inner voice, the voice that we “hear” when we read to ourselves. All reading is therefore a vocal performance, generally just for an audience of one. Consider how much the language of reading and writing supports this notion: We talk about an author’s voice and tone and rhythm. We ask, “What is the author trying to say?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22BLACK1/22BLACK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22BLACK1/22BLACK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22BLACK1/22BLACK1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22BLACK1/22BLACK1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1578, 'width': 1578, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22BLACK1/22BLACK1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22BLACK1/22BLACK1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/books/review/22BLACK1/22BLACK1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kurt Von