5-11-20T17:32:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Sciolino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sciolino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5400210a-9cc0-5a86-b574-172c61120b98', 'word_count': 867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5400210a-9cc0-5a86-b574-172c61120b98'}, {'abstract': 'A group of veterans start a volunteer organization for soldiers who have returned to civilian life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/books/review/joe-kleins-charlie-mike.html', 'snippet': 'A group of veterans start a volunteer organization for soldiers who have returned to civilian life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kenneth Harbaugh, a former Navy pilot, believes that humor can be “wrung from the most awful things.” As he recalls, one of his fellow veterans, a double amputee, used to laugh about losing “$300 worth of tattoos.” But the joke turned somber when Harbaugh’s friend added that what he regretted most wasn’t the loss of his tattoos or limbs; it was that “he didn’t stop enough shrapnel with his own body to save his squad mate from getting hit.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Joe Klein’s ‘Charlie Mike’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beyond the Call of Duty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klein, Joe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Charlie Mike: A True Story of Heroes Who Brought Their Mission Home (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T17:32:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'sect