in Bamako, the capital of Mali, on Friday, taking about 100 people hostage and killing more than two dozen. The attack is the latest eruption of violence in Mali, a former French colony in West Africa that has been racked by an Islamist insurgency for the past three years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/21/world/21BAMAKO2/21BAMAKO2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/21/world/21BAMAKO2/21BAMAKO2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/21/world/21BAMAKO2/21BAMAKO2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/21/world/21BAMAKO2/21BAMAKO2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1303, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/21/world/21BAMAKO2/21BAMAKO2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/21/world/21BAMAKO2/21BAMAKO2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/21/world/21BAMAKO2/21BAMAKO2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What We Know and Don’t Know About the Terrorist Attack in Mali', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mali', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T19:22:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Russell Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Russell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d93cd27e-2aee-5f1c-ab01-80fdb4af8339', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d93cd27e-2aee-5f1c-ab01-80fdb4af8339'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Taylor, the author of “The Up South Cookbook: Chasing Dixie in a Brooklyn Kitchen,” seeks out Southern flavors as she dines in and out over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/nyregion/how-nicole-a-taylor-cookbook-author-spends-her-sundays.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Taylor, the author of “The Up South Cookbook: Chasing Dixie in a Brooklyn Kitchen,” seeks out Southern flavors as she dines in and out over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In college, a friend of Nicole A. Taylor’s called her “the black Betty Crocker,” which is not a bad catchphrase to recall when you’re promoting a new cookbook during the holidays. A Georgia native, she is the author of “The Up South Cookbook: Chasing Dixie in a Brooklyn Kitchen” and the host of the podcast “Hot Grease,” in which she tells stories related to Southern food. Ms. Taylor, 37, who said Southern cooking didn’t become part of her career until she moved to New York City in 2008, lives with her husband, Adrian Franks, 39, an artist and a user-experience design director at IBM, in Bedford-Stuyvesant, Brooklyn.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/nyregion/22ROUTINE1/22ROUTINE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Nicole A. Taylor, Cookbook Author, Spends Her Sundays', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gospel or Jazz, Then Roast a Bird', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Correal, Annie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Nicole A (1978- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Up Sou