rance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanders, Bernard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Memphis (Tenn)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nashville (Tenn)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T22:13:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b90045f-282f-5fa1-bd76-8421f2c4a9cc', 'word_count': 393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b90045f-282f-5fa1-bd76-8421f2c4a9cc'}, {'abstract': 'Our senses have a big impact on which words thrive and which fizzle out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/opinion/why-cool-is-still-cool.html', 'snippet': 'Our senses have a big impact on which words thrive and which fizzle out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE word “cool” has been cool for a long time. Originally associated with temperature, by the 16th century the term had evolved to describe not just the atmosphere, but also an internal state of calm, almost icy composure. And by the late 1800s it began to signify style and hipness and some of the other meanings with which it is associated today. Now cool is used as a synonym for almost anything good. Music can be cool and restaurants can be cool. Every so often even a minivan seems cool.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22gray/22gray-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 456, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22gray/22gray-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 456}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22gray/22gray-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 779, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22gray/22gray-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1300, 'width': 1709, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22gray/22gray-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22gray/22gray-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22gray/22gray-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why ‘Cool’ Is Still Cool', 'kicker': 'Gray Matter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why ‘Cool’ Is Still Cool', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Senses and Sensation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T22:13:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonah Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c2bf70a-f3ea-53e0-9427-574bca0f82b5', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c2bf70a-f3ea-53e0-9427-574bca0f82b5'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Murney’s untitled show at Feinstein’s/54 Below includes a sing-along with four-letter-words but no selections from “Wicked.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/21/arts/music/review-julia-murney-in-honesty-mode-at-feinsteins-54-below.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Murney’s untitled show at Feinstein’s/54 Below includes a sing-along with four-letter-words but no selections from “Wicked.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Julia Murney doesn’t mince words. The high point of her untitled opening-night show at Feinstein’s/54 Below on Thursday was a sing-along rendition of Sara Bareilles’s “Sweet as Whole.” It was prefaced by an anecdote about a man urinating in a New York subway station, Ms. Murney’s disgust and another observer’s mean-spirited reaction.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Julia Murney in Honesty Mode at Feinstein’s/54 Below', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cursing Silently in the City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '54 Below (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murney, Julia K (1969- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T22:23:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/998814bd-f409-530f-8c77-171e6c00386b', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/998814bd-f409-530f-8c77-171e6c00386b'}, {'abstract': 'With the attacks in Paris, ISIS has declared war on the ‘‘gray zone.’’ But the language of some American politicians is also questioning coexistence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/magazine/my-life-as-a-muslim-in-the-wests-gray-zone.html', 'snippet': 'With the attacks in Paris, ISIS has declared war on the ‘‘gray zone.’’ But the language of some American politicians is also questioning coexistence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some months ago, I gave a reading from my most recent novel in Scottsdale, Ariz. During the discussion that followed, a woman asked me to talk about my upbringing in Morocco. It’s natural for readers to be curious about a writer they’ve come to hear, I told myself. I continued to tell myself this even after the conversation drifted to Islam, and then to ISIS. Eventually, another woman raised her hand and said that the only Muslims she saw when she turned on the television were extremists. “Why aren’t we hearing more from people like you?” she asked me.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29firstwords/29firstwords-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29firstwords/29firstwords-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29firstwords/29firstwords-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29firstwords/29firstwords-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29firstwords/29firstwords-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29firstwords/29firstwords-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29firstwords/29firstwords-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Life as a Muslim in the West’s ‘Gray Zone’', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For or Against', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lalami, Laila', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-20T22:27:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laila Lalami', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 