n', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7df5ec0f-a9b8-585d-90ce-1157d77b9e0e', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7df5ec0f-a9b8-585d-90ce-1157d77b9e0e'}, {'abstract': 'Saudi Arabia’s single-minded preoccupation with its regional rival is diverting attention from other pressing issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/21/opinion/what-the-saudis-miss-when-they-focus-on-iran.html', 'snippet': 'Saudi Arabia’s single-minded preoccupation with its regional rival is diverting attention from other pressing issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RIYADH, Saudi Arabia — It doesn’t take long in Saudi Arabia to see evidence of an obsession with Iran, the Shiite foil to this Sunni country. In recent conversations, government officials, analysts and members of the royal family were quick to describe how incensed they were that President Obama hadn’t found a way to remove Bashar al-Assad, the Syrian president and Iran’s close ally, from power.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/21/opinion/21sat3web/21sat3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/21/opinion/21sat3web/21sat3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/21/opinion/21sat3web/21sat3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/21/opinion/21sat3web/21sat3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 