ticle', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Vlasic and Mary M. Chapman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vlasic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/afa8a51a-5b73-5667-89bf-873b1968bf1a', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afa8a51a-5b73-5667-89bf-873b1968bf1a'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon make me work for my cryptic fix, and the passing of Rosalie Moscovitch.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/21/variety-a-tough-cryptic/', 'snippet': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon make me work for my cryptic fix, and the passing of Rosalie Moscovitch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VARIETY PUZZLE — Before we get started, I wanted to take a moment to mention the passing of Rosalie Moscovitch, who contributed cryptics and other variety puzzles to The New York Times. I never had a chance to meet her in person, but we corresponded behind the scenes. Ms. Moscovitch died Nov. 10 and she will be missed. If you would like to try her last variety puzzle that she co-wrote with Joe Cabrera, you can find it here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: A Tough Cryptic', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-21T17:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/202080c8-7f36-54f8-b3b5-80c4ff644c8e', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/202080c8-7f36-54f8-b3b5-80c4ff644c8e'}, {'abstract': 'Jessica and Luke Cage learn a little more about each other.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/20/arts/television/jessica-jones-episode-2-the-devil-did.html', 'snippet': 'Jessica and Luke Cage learn a little more about each other.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Control is everything in another thoughtfully character-driven, albeit tonally wonky episode of “Jessica Jones.” We are constantly reminded of the show’s thematic concern with control through dialogue that underscores the way that everyone, from supervillain Kilgrave to good guy Luke Cage, struggles to stay on top of their lives. Jones knows that struggle well, as we see when she nurses a drink while investigating Kilgrave’s case. She snaps at her upstairs neighbors for the same reason: She can’t see the world beyond her own problems, and therefore despises people she imagines can’t handle their own private lives — in private.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/television/13jessica2/13jessica2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/television/13jessica2/13jessica2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/television/13jessica2/13jessica2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/13/arts/television/13jessica2/13jessica2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048,