 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Arthur C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emotions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-21T19:30:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arthur C. Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arthur', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/faa4fa88-ce20-5bdc-9de6-501ae19e4277', 'word_count': 1384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/faa4fa88-ce20-5bdc-9de6-501ae19e4277'}, {'abstract': 'The most boring, most important, issues in politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/ruling-from-the-shadows.html', 'snippet': 'The most boring, most important, issues in politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WE’VE come to expect bickering from our politicians. We’re often exasperated by this state of affairs and sometimes we yearn for a quieter, gentler age, when compromise wasn’t a dirty word. But the raucous rivalry in Washington has its benefits: a political process thick with competition. By contrast, political competition in other important parts of our democracy is worrisomely thin.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22RAMANNA/22ramanna-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/22/opinion/sunday/22RAMANNA/22ramanna-