bject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-23T05:28:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Forsythe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Forsythe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc5f915c-a2bc-5a36-a0c6-ff71fcebf6b5', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc5f915c-a2bc-5a36-a0c6-ff71fcebf6b5'}, {'abstract': 'Syrian refugees seeking safe haven in Europe have taken to the Arctic Circle, where they cycle the final stretch across the border from Russia into Norway.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/23/syrian-refugees-biking-to-safety/', 'snippet': 'Syrian refugees seeking safe haven in Europe have taken to the Arctic Circle, where they cycle the final stretch across the border from Russia into Norway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid all the dramatic stories of terrified Syrian refugees seeking safe haven in Europe, one odd detail struck Alessandro Iovino: People were riding bicycles along a new path to freedom through the Arctic Circle. Their choice of transport was an ingenious response to a vexing 390-foot final stretch along the border between Russia and Norway. The Russians banned foot traffic, while the Norwegians prohibited vehicles carrying people without visas.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Syrian Refugees Biking to Safety', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and Africa Migrant Crisis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norway', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-23T08:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rena Silverman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silverman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7791ea9f-aec0-52f8-8fae-081eafe816f4', 'word_count': 733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7791ea9f-aec0-52f8-8fae-081eafe816f4'}, {'abstract': 'It’s no small victory to hear Democrats and one Republican presidential contender talk about support for working parents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/23/opinion/campaign-stops/the-politics-of-paid-time-off-to-have-a-baby.html', 'snippet': 'It’s no small victory to hear Democrats and one Republican presidential contender talk about support for working parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1980, when Hillary Rodham Clinton was about to become a mother, she was working as a partner at Rose Law Firm in Arkansas. At the time, paid maternity leave, or leave at all, was a rare benefit. Her firm, she has said, had no maternity leave plan until she told her partners she would be taking four months off, thus establishing its first policy. Just five of the top 30 employers for women, as selected by Working Mother magazine when it first started the list in the 1980s, offered paid maternity leave, and those that did gave between one week and eight, often only at partial pay.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23covertWeb/23covertWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23covertWeb/23covertWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 440}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23covertWeb/23covertWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23covertWeb/23covertWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1503, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23covertWeb/23covertWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23covertWeb/23covertWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23covertWeb/23covertWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Politics of Paid Time Off to Have a Baby', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Family Leaves', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paid Time Off', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Work-Life Balance', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubio, Marco', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-23T08:19:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Campaign Stops', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bryce Covert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bryce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Covert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/51536041-99aa-55e1-a706-7d8d3c6939d7', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51536041-99aa-55e1-a706-7d8d3c6939d7'}, {'abstract': 'There has been some negative news lately about Obamacare, but it is still a big success story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/23/opinion/health-reform-lives.html', 'snippet': 'There has been some negative news lately about Obamacare, but it is still a big success story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the right’s dismay, scare tactics — remember death panels? — and spurious legal challenges failed to protect the nation from the scourge of guaranteed health coverage. Still, Obamacare’s opponents insisted that it would implode in a “death spiral” of low enrollment and rising costs.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 591, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/12/opinion/krugman-circular/krugman-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 591}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't