', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Rovane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rovane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2b7fcf1-11db-582e-a018-e8952d5f5bb4', 'word_count': 1687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2b7fcf1-11db-582e-a018-e8952d5f5bb4'}, {'abstract': 'First responders are still relying on an emergency system based on dangerously outmoded technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/23/opinion/the-911-system-isnt-ready-for-the-iphone-era.html', 'snippet': 'First responders are still relying on an emergency system based on dangerously outmoded technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1999, Congress established 911 as the nationwide emergency number, and called for a system that would use the best technology available to deliver emergency assistance. Now, 16 years later, our emergency response system faces an emergency of its own in the form of outdated technology. To fix this, our emergency responders — police, fire and ambulance — urgently need the help of government leaders at all levels.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23wheeler/23wheeler-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 802, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23wheeler/23wheeler-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 802}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/opinion/23wheeler/23wheeler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'cr