'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03cd790b-e3dd-56e1-9ac1-d0ecfb117ce6', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03cd790b-e3dd-56e1-9ac1-d0ecfb117ce6'}, {'abstract': 'A new biography of Gore Vidal, novelist, playwright, screenwriter, social and political player.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/books/review/empire-of-self-a-life-of-gore-vidal-by-jay-parini.html', 'snippet': 'A new biography of Gore Vidal, novelist, playwright, screenwriter, social and political player.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Easily the best, most entertaining book about Gore Vidal is his 1995 memoir “Palimpsest.” But with the possible exception of “In Bed With Gore Vidal,” Tim Teeman’s 2013 tell-all, “Palimpsest” is also the least reliable of the Vidal books. Vidal was a tireless self-mythologizer, and as his title suggests, that book is a layering of rememberings, re-rememberings and mis-rememberings. In his new, much sounder biography, “Empire of Self,” Jay Parini suggests that even the account of Vidal’s idyllic romance with his high school friend Jimmie Trimble, one of the touchstones of “Palimpsest” — the story of a love so perfect and unearthly that it could never be duplicated — was most likely a fabrication.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MCGRATH/MCGRATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MCGRATH/MCGRATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 568}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MCGRATH/MCGRATH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 969, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MCGRATH/MCGRATH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1939, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MCGRATH/1129MCGRATH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MCGRATH/1129MCGRATH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MCGRATH/1129MCGRATH-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Empire of Self: A Life of Gore Vidal,’ by Jay Parini', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Best Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGrath, Charles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parini, Jay', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vidal, Gore', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Empire of Self: A Life of Gore Vidal (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-23T10:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf392a1c-2467-517e-ac8c-821b951d36b9', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf392a1c-2467-517e-ac8c-821b951d36b9'}, {'abstract': 'Three volumes bring into focus the breadth and coherence of Levi’s genius.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/books/review/the-complete-works-of-primo-levi.html', 'snippet': 'Three volumes bring into focus the breadth and coherence of Levi’s genius.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Primo Levi studied chemistry at Turin and worked as a chemist until, at 24, he joined the Italian “partisans” resisting the Nazi occupation of northern Italy in 1943. He was arrested by Italian Fascists and turned over to the Germans, who sent him to Auschwitz — he called it the Lager, the German word for a concentration camp — where he survived partly by luck, partly because he was put to work in a synthetic-rubber factory that used prisoners as slave labor. Returning to Italy, he wrote his memoir of Auschwitz, “If This Is a Man” (1947), and worked 30 years for a paint factory while writing stories, poems, memoirs, essays, a novel and “The Periodic Table” (1975), his idiosyncratic autobiography in which each chapter was named for a chemical element and some chapters were short stories.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MENDELSON/1129MENDELSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 865, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MENDELSON/1129MENDELSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 865}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129MENDELSON/1129MENDELSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 711, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': 