alists on Monday prevented repair crews from restoring the main power lines in southern Ukraine that supply Crimea, leaving the disputed peninsula in the dark and Ukraine and Russia headed toward a standoff over the issue.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Sabotage Blacks Out Crimea, Tatars Prevent Repairs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Activists Block Repair of Sabotaged Lines in Crimea, Leaving Region in Darkness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Crimea (Ukraine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tatars (Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Power Failures and Blackouts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sabotage (Crime)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Right Sector (Ukraine)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poroshenko, Petro Olekseyevich', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-23T14:52:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ivan Nechepurenko and Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ivan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nechepurenko', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6263bf52-81ec-5759-96e8-0990722d9c37', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6263bf52-81ec-5759-96e8-0990722d9c37'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Russia begins to regard the use of nuclear weapons as not inevitable in future wars in 1965.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/23/1965-russia-shifts-position-on-a-war/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: Russia begins to regard the use of nuclear weapons as not inevitable in future wars in 1965.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — The weight of Soviet military thinking is apparently shifting toward the position that nuclear weapons would not inevitably be used in a future war, qualified sources reported today [Nov. 23]. This departure from the opposite view identified with Nikita S. Khrushchev, that the immediate or early use of nuclear arms had to be expected, was cited as the most meaningful change in Soviet strategy in the year since the former premier fell. It is a welcome change to the United States, which made a similar shift itself about five years ago.\xa0— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, November 24, 1965', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1965: Russia Shifts Position on A-war', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-23T15:13:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribun