rly sure I’d left in the back of the closet.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29lor/29lor-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29lor/29lor-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29lor/29lor-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29lor/29lor-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29lor/29lor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29lor/29lor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/magazine/29lor/29lor-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Letter of Recommendation: Kitchen Timer', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kitchen Timer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Productivity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dolnick, Ben', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freelancing, Self-Employment and Independent Contracting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cookware', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Time', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-24T12:53:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Dolnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dolnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35680b3e-7d7b-594d-ab08-13413aa3c9d9', 'word_count': 950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35680b3e-7d7b-594d-ab08-13413aa3c9d9'}, {'abstract': 'When the temperature drops, no one can argue against the need for a hat. But is it possible to find a way to wear one that isn’t ridiculous?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/magazine/the-winter-hat-trick.html', 'snippet': 'When the temperature drops, no one can argue against the need for a hat. But is it possible to find a way to wear one that isn’t ridiculous?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here they come now, winter hats, making their annual flight from the recesses of our closets to the crests of our persons. The plain-\xadspoken stocking caps, the trapper hats, the rabbit-\xadfur fantasia — to watch these and a hundred other designs re-\xadenter circulation is at once to witness the most natural thing in the world and to size up something strange. Hats seem essential in frigid air, but many seem \xadimpossible to wear with a straight face, given the cultural climate. Get a whiff of the models that served our grandfathers well: They now combine the must of a midcentury hatbox with the musk of 21st-\xadcentury body spray. Take a look at the grown-\xadups wearing wool caps decorated to the verge of doubling as stuffed animals. The impish self-\xadconsciousness of such nonsense shows that gussying up the head is, however awkward, serious business.', 'print_secti