said. It was the first such attack under the country’s leftist-led government, fueling fears of a possible resurgence of domestic terrorism.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Athens Blast Hurts None but Fuels Fears of Domestic Terrorism', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bombing in Athens Fuels Fears of Domestic Terrorism ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Athens (Greece)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kitsantonis, Niki', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsipras, Alexis', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eurozone', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-24T13:47:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niki Kitsantonis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niki', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kitsantonis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bf55952-05ff-51ca-9767-d694583957ea', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bf55952-05ff-51ca-9767-d694583957ea'}, {'abstract': 'Just don’t expect that anyone’s mind will be changed by the time dessert is served.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/25/upshot/how-to-talk-to-your-relatives-about-politics-at-thanksgiving.html', 'snippet': 'Just don’t expect that anyone’s mind will be changed by the time dessert is served.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s time for that annual American holiday tradition: awkward political conversation at the Thanksgiving dinner table. With the 2016 presidential primary campaign in full swing and public interest on the rise, the odds are good that relatives will share their thoughts with you about why one candidate will win or how another is going to destroy America. I can’t tell you how to keep your family away from sensitive topics, but here are brief answers to some frequently asked questions about current events and the 2016 race.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/upshot/00_nyhan_test_jody/00_nyhan_test_jody-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/23/upshot/00_nyhan_test_jody/00_nyhan_test_jody-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/upshot/00_nyhan_test_jody/00_nyhan_test_jody-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 525, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/upshot/00_nyhan_test_jody/00_nyhan_test_jody-superJumbo.gif', 'height': 1049, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/upshot/00_nyhan_test_jody/00_nyhan_test_jody-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/23/upshot/00_nyhan_test_jody/00_nyhan_test_jody-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/23/upshot/00_nyhan_test_jody/00_nyhan_test_jody-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Talk to Your Relatives About Politics at Thanksgiving', 'kicker': 'Chewing the Fat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-24T14:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brendan Nyhan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brendan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nyhan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fea214cb-5653-5e0f-a42f-ab28ef47048f', 'word_count': 579, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fea214cb-5653-5e0f-a42f-ab28ef47048f'}, {'abstract': 'Seven decades on, it is remarkable how many of our political arguments continue to focus on the resistance of Nazi-like behavior, whether in ourselves or others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/24/us/in-arguments-over-terrorism-echoes-of-world-war-ii.html', 'snippet': 'Seven decades on, it is remarkable how many of our political arguments continue to focus on the resistance of Nazi-like behavior, whether in ourselves or others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It gets your attention when a leader says we are at “the beginning of a terrorist world war.” It carries greater weight when that leader runs a major intelligence agency. And more weight still when that agency is German, its very name a product of the last world war and its lessons about fear and evil and the terrifying ease of losing one’s soul: the Federal Office for the Protection of the Constitution.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge',