: 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/24/world/middleeast/russia-turkey-jet-shoot-down-maps-1448382166586/russia-turkey-jet-shoot-down-maps-1448382166586-thumbStandard-v3.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/24/world/middleeast/russia-turkey-jet-shoot-down-maps-1448382166586/russia-turkey-jet-shoot-down-maps-1448382166586-thumbLarge-v2.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sorting Out What Russia and Turkey Say Happened in the Sky', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War and Revolution', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Aircraft', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-24T16:24:12+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By THE NEW YORK TIMES', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/cfcaa6ad-42e3-5b24-9c87-4bd172d2161f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/cfcaa6ad-42e3-5b24-9c87-4bd172d2161f'}, {'abstract': 'Dawkins’s second memoir plumbs his grown-up research, reflections and controversies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/books/review/brief-candle-in-the-dark-by-richard-dawkins.html', 'snippet': 'Dawkins’s second memoir plumbs his grown-up research, reflections and controversies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some lumbering robot, this Richard Dawkins. “Lumbering robots” was one of the ways in which this scarily brilliant evolutionary biologist described human beings vis-à-vis their genes in “The Selfish Gene,” his first and probably still his most influential book — more than a million copies sold. (His atheist manifesto, “The God Delusion,” has sold more than three million.) We’re essentially a means of physical and, more important, temporal transportation for our genes, he explained. They can live on for eons after we take our own inherited genes and mate with those of that handsome boy behind us in the \xadmovie-ticket line who ended up sitting next to us or the ones belonging to that pretty girl whose change we picked up by mistake at the newsstand and with whom we then had an apologetic coffee. And so on down the line. Our lines. Dawkins has also called us “throwaway survival machines” for our genes. But only, I think, to make a biological point.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/books/review/27menaker/27menaker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/27/books/review/27menaker/27menaker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/books/review/27menaker/27menaker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/books/review/27menaker/27menaker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/books/review/27menaker/27menaker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/27/books/review/27menaker/27menaker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/books/review/27menaker/27menaker-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Brief Candle in the Dark,’ by Richard Dawkins', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In His Genes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dawkins, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Brief Candle in the Dark (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-24T16:24:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Menaker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Menaker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/866014b0-557b-5417-be23-c5fef6667469', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/866014b0-557b-5417-be23-c5fef6667469'}, {'abstract': 'An actress and a curator plumb the depths of what we wear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/24/t-magazine/tilda-swinton-olivier-saillard-art-of-dressing-book.html', 'snippet': 'An actress and a curator plumb the depths of what we wear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the last few years, Tilda Swinton has collaborated with the fashion curator Olivier Saillard on three rather unusual live performances, now documented in a catalog called ‘‘Impossible Wardrobes.’’ In one piece, Saillard and Swinton made a dress to measure, with Swinton as a dress form. For another, the actress painstakingly carried historically significant items of clothing, like Napoleon’s coat, down a runway. ‘‘In many ways,’’ she explains, ‘‘the clothes operated as a bridge to the person no longer inside them — almost as if the garment is a symptom or a leaf shed from a tree.’’ In the final work, Swinton assumed the role of coat-check attendant, leaving bits of personal ephemera, like a drop of perfume or a lipstick-smeared tissue, in visitors’ pockets. ‘‘I still prize the old shoes and tiny cardigans of my now sky-scraping children,’’ Swinton says. ‘‘They link to the past like portals.’’', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/12/t-magazine/12tmag-tilda-t/12tmag-tilda-t-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/12/t-magazine/12tmag-tilda-t/12tmag-tilda-t-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/12/t-magazine/12tmag-tilda-t/12tmag-tilda-t-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/12/t-magazine/12tmag-tilda-t/12tmag-tilda-t-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1393, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/12/t-magazine/12tmag-tilda-t/12tmag-tilda-t-thumbStandar