ible for the extinction of dinosaurs and the subsequent rise of mammals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/books/review/dark-matter-and-the-dinosaurs-by-lisa-randall.html', 'snippet': 'Dark matter, according to Lisa Randall, may be responsible for the extinction of dinosaurs and the subsequent rise of mammals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A good theory is an act of the informed imagination — it reaches toward the unknown while grounded in the firmest foundations of the known. In “Dark Matter and the Dinosaurs,” the Harvard cosmologist Lisa Randall proposes that a thin disk of dark matter in the plane of the Milky Way triggered a minor perturbation in deep space that caused the major earthly catastrophe that decimated the dinosaurs. It’s an original theory that builds on a century of groundbreaking discoveries to tell the story of how the universe as we know it came to exist, how dark matter illuminates its beguiling unknowns and how the physics of elementary particles, the physics of space, and the biology of life intertwine in ways both bewildering and profound.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/29POPOVA/1129-BKS-Popova-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 562, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/29POPOVA/1129-BKS-Popova-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 562}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/29POPOVA/1129-BKS-Popova-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/29POPOVA/1129-BKS-Popova-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1920, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/29POPOVA/29POPOVA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/29POPOVA/29POPOVA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/29POPOVA/29POPOVA-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Dark Matter and the Dinosaurs,’ by Lisa Randall', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Force of Impact', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Randall, Lisa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dark Matter and the Dinosaurs: The Astounding Interconnectedness of the Universe (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-24T17:43:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Popova', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Popova', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c78de668-5520-511f-b432-27af786a859c', 'word_count': 1311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c78de668-5520-511f-b432-27af786a859c'}, {'abstract': 'The first volume of a new history of modern Europe covers two world wars and the troubled years between them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/books/review/ian-kershaws-to-hell-and-back-europe-1914-1949.html', 'snippet': 'The first volume of a new history of modern Europe covers two world wars and the troubled years between them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ian Kershaw’s chilling epic-size history produces aftershocks connected to our current news headlines. Polish soldiers close border. .\u2008.\u2008. Rise of Austrian right. .\u2008.\u2008. Grim jobs report. .\u2008.\u2008. Shocking British vote. .\u2008.\u2008. Germans want sanctions against Russia. .\u2008.\u2008. Europe migrant crisis boils over. .\u2008.\u2008. They are all so reminiscent of the drum rolls that preceded Europe’s descent into “the pit of barbarism” during a century of two world wars that almost destroyed its civilization, followed by 40 years of Cold War. Seventy years after the democracies, together with the Red Army, triumphed in World War II, and 25 years after the reunification of Germany, the new Europe has made what Kershaw deems an “astonishing recovery.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129EVANS/EVANS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129EVANS/EVANS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129EVANS/EVANS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 619, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129EVANS/EVANS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1238, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {