name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Rights Watch', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-25T08:59:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdb58230-04e9-59d4-ad7a-7cec09c91ffb', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdb58230-04e9-59d4-ad7a-7cec09c91ffb'}, {'abstract': 'The Swedish company is being taken public by its private equity owner EQT Partners and was valued at about $1.6 billion in the offering.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/26/business/dealbook/dometic-group-raises-528-million-in-ipo-in-stockholm.html', 'snippet': 'The Swedish company is being taken public by its private equity owner EQT Partners and was valued at about $1.6 billion in the offering.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Dometic Group, a Swedish maker of appliances for boats and recreational vehicles, said on Wednesday that it had raised 4.6 billion Swedish kronor, or about $528 million, in its initial public offering in Stockholm.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dometic Group Raises $528 Million in I.P.O. in Stockholm', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'EQT Funds Management Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recreational Vehicles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Stockholm (Sweden)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-25T09:07:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2009dc3f-9d33-5987-b4df-42ac2d872516', 'word_count': 354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2009dc3f-9d33-5987-b4df-42ac2d872516'}, {'abstract': 'The sport has never seriously considered outlawing the bouncer, even though Phillip Hughes of Australia died after he was struck in the neck by a ball.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/26/sports/cricket/a-year-after-players-death-cricket-phillip-hughes.html', 'snippet': 'The sport has never seriously considered outlawing the bouncer, even though Phillip Hughes of Australia died after he was struck in the neck by a ball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Nov. 25 last year, the Australian cricketer Phillip Hughes was attempting to win a recall to the test-playing national team. He had reached 63 runs during a domestic match in Sydney, Australia, when he received a short ball aimed near his head. Trying to attack the delivery, Hughes was struck on the top of the neck and, after staggering for a few seconds, he collapsed on the field. Within 48 hours he was pronounced dead, three days before his 26th birthday.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/25/sports/26cricket/26cricket-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/25/sports/26cricket/26cricket-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype'