', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia and Ukraine in a Standoff Over Crimea Power Outage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia and Ukraine in a Standoff Over Crimean Power Cutoff', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Crimea (Ukraine)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gazprom', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tatars (Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Power Failures and Blackouts', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yatsenyuk, Arseniy P', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-26T00:49:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alisa Sopova and Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sopova', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7a8659d-3e1b-59cf-b8b6-787ef8ba3264', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7a8659d-3e1b-59cf-b8b6-787ef8ba3264'}, {'abstract': 'We’ve arrived at a point where not only is reality stranger than fiction, but we don’t allow our fiction to be even close to how strange real life is every day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/29/books/review/highly-unlikely.html', 'snippet': 'We’ve arrived at a point where not only is reality stranger than fiction, but we don’t allow our fiction to be even close to how strange real life is every day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When something extraordinary happens, we often say it’s stranger than fiction. But reality routinely, every minute of every day, outdoes all realist fiction in its strangeness. Recently two women working on the same floor of a Florida hospital discovered they were sisters (they had been adopted by different families in the 1970s). We read this item in the newspaper and accept it as astonishing, but real. The same occurrence in a realist novel, though, would be called unlikely or unbelievable. We’ve arrived at a point where not only is reality stranger than fiction, but we don’t allow our fiction to be even close to how strange real life is every day.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 572, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1143, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/29/books/review/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote/1129-BKS-AuthorsNote-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Highly Unlikely', 'kicker': 'Author’s Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Highly Unlikely', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-26T00:50:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'Vendela Vida', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vendela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vida', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/197b5f62-116f-5b47-aaea-733627826df6', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/197b5f62-116f-5b47-aaea-733627826df6'}, {'abstract': 'I thought I wanted to celebrate the holiday on my own terms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/26/opinion/freeing-thanksgiving-from-my-family.html', 'snippet': 'I thought I wanted to celebrate the holiday on my own terms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT was a few hours before dawn, and the cartoon characters were in sad shape. There they lay, half inflated in the streets encircling New York City’s Museum of Natural History: Snoopy and Hello Kitty and Kermit the Frog and Superman. They were tethered to the ground with giant nets, like something