a (Deepika Padukone) on vacation in Corsica. Their largely chaste relationship revolves around playacting, and they avoid exchanging real names. When Tara returns to India, they decide to never meet again, their idyll forever in amber.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/arts/27TAMASHA/27TAMASHA-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/27/arts/27TAMASHA/27TAMASHA-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 467}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/arts/27TAMASHA/27TAMASHA-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/arts/27TAMASHA/27TAMASHA-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1593, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/arts/27TAMASHA/27TAMASHA-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/11/27/arts/27TAMASHA/27TAMASHA-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/27/arts/27TAMASHA/27TAMASHA-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Tamasha,’ Finding a Path to Love, Show Business and Adulation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Review: In ‘Tamasha,’ Finding a Path to Love, Show Business and Adulation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ali, Imtiaz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-26T21:37:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/20122768-115a-5ac7-aa22-5dd08ff59945', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20122768-115a-5ac7-aa22-5dd08ff59945'}, {'abstract': '‘Agnosia, an Illuminated Ontology,’ an installation by the Conceptual artist, features words and phrases in neon and other lighting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/27/arts/design/joseph-kosuth-show-at-sean-kelly-gallery.html', 'snippet': '‘Agnosia, an Illuminated Ontology,’ an installation by the Conceptual artist, features words and phrases in neon and other lighting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 1950s and ’60s were peak years for “the linguistic turn,” a philosophical movement that looked to the analysis of language for solutions to traditional puzzles about consciousness and reality. Few artists were more profoundly affected by that trend than Joseph Kosuth, a pioneer of ’60s-style Conceptualism. “Agnosia, an Illuminated Ontology,” a visually exciting and conceptually bookish installation designed by Mr. Kosuth, gathers together more than 40 works from 1965 to 2015 that feature electrically illuminated words, phrases and sentences. (Agnosia is an inability to make sense of sensory information, a condition usually caused by brain damage.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty