document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2aa7c5e2-5b19-5024-b4ef-3e874670f495', 'word_count': 1400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2aa7c5e2-5b19-5024-b4ef-3e874670f495'}, {'abstract': 'Three decades ago Steve DeOssie and Jim Jeffcoat became friends, as did their wives. Each couple had twins, one boy and one girl apiece. Now the two boys play for the N.F.L., while the girls are co-workers in Colorado.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/28/sports/football/steve-deossie-jim-jeffcoat-zak-deossie-jackson-jeffcoat.html', 'snippet': 'Three decades ago Steve DeOssie and Jim Jeffcoat became friends, as did their wives. Each couple had twins, one boy and one girl apiece. Now the two boys play for the N.F.L., while the girls are co-workers in Colorado.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a rookie linebacker and long snapper with the Dallas Cowboys in 1984, Steve DeOssie assumed a mentality of being “crazy all the time.” A second-year teammate, however, made an important impression. Defensive end Jim Jeffcoat showed DeOssie that it was possible to assume a separate mentality off the field. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/28/sports/Y-JP-TWINS/Y-JP-TWINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/28/sports/Y-JP-TWINS/Y-JP-TWINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/28/sports/Y-JP-TWINS/Y-JP-TW