1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fitzpatrick, Ryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Dolphins', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-29T22:02:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d09c1b38-bba9-5048-a669-0995ed02a4b3', 'word_count': 1084, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d09c1b38-bba9-5048-a669-0995ed02a4b3'}, {'abstract': 'The original trio members Dom Salvador and Sérgio Barroso were joined by Duduka da Fonseca, who took the place of Edison Machado, who died in 1990.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/30/arts/music/review-the-rio-65-trio-resurrects-the-barreling-sounds-of-a-samba-jazz-band.html', 'snippet': 'The original trio members Dom Salvador and Sérgio Barroso were joined by Duduka da Fonseca, who took the place of Edison Machado, who died in 1990.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years after the invention of bossa nova in Brazil, in the late 1950s, there appeared a wave of samba-jazz groups, often piano trios. The two styles are linked at the root by samba rhythm, but they can feel like opposites. The best bossa nova was light, sensitive, skeptical, measured, full of silence. The samba-jazz of the early ’60s was dense and fast and barreling, and the Rio 65 Trio, who made two records in 1965 and 1966, embodied that sound.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/30/arts/30RIO65TRIO/30RIO65TRIO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 