-308454510a9c'}, {'abstract': 'This revival of Susan Glaspell’s 1930 play, loosely based on events surrounding Emily Dickinson, provides an opportunity to reflect on defending women’s freedom in a man’s world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/30/theater/review-in-alisons-house-a-family-struggles-to-protect-a-literary-legacy.html', 'snippet': 'This revival of Susan Glaspell’s 1930 play, loosely based on events surrounding Emily Dickinson, provides an opportunity to reflect on defending women’s freedom in a man’s world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are many lenses through which to view “Alison’s House,” Susan Glaspell’s 1930 drama exploring a family’s angst over the literary output of a beloved dead relative. You could examine Glaspell’s early-20th-century feminist politics, or meditate on how the competing demands of celebrity and privacy have and have not shifted in the 85 years between its premiere and the current production, directed by Alex Roe at the Metropolitan Playhouse.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/30/arts/30ALISON/30ALISON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 473, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/11/30/arts/30ALISON/30ALISON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 473}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/30/arts/30ALISON/30ALISON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 807, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/11/30/arts/30ALISON/30ALISON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1613, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '