, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Associated Press', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Post', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-30T17:23:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cef72dbc-c60a-5d19-abfd-fd7b1e67b4bc', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cef72dbc-c60a-5d19-abfd-fd7b1e67b4bc'}, {'abstract': 'An English writer’s account of living in the Mississippi Delta, a filmmaker’s travels in South America and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/12/06/books/review/travel-dispatches-from-pluto-driving-hungry-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'An English writer’s account of living in the Mississippi Delta, a filmmaker’s travels in South America and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is no such place as untrammeled earth. A journey to any spot on the globe is certain to cross the traces of past civilizations, the residue of different eras. They may announce themselves in wreckages of marble or stone, or they may have discreetly sunk beneath the dirt. Yet wherever we may travel, the ghosts of history beckon. They remind us of our predecessors and also of our own transience. The essentially changing character of nature means that we never have true ownership of any patch of ground; we’re all just renting for a season before we’re forced to move on. In their own different ways, the authors of these new travel narratives pay tribute to the earth’s earlier guests.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'mu