albums return to the Top 20.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/11/30/adele-25-chart-justin-bieber/', 'snippet': '“Purpose” slips to second place as “25” breaks records at No. 1 and Adele’s two previous albums return to the Top 20.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The record-crushing sales for Adele’s “25” accounted for 41 percent of all album purchases in the United States last week — and 48 percent of all digital album sales, according to Nielsen Music. But there were, in fact, many other releases available.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justin Bieber Charts at No. 2 in a Week That’s All About Adele', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Albums Adele Left in the Dust With u201825u2019', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adele (Singer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bieber, Justin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tesfaye, Abel (Weeknd)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'N Sync', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-30T17:41:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Coscarelli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coscarelli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80e44421-a5be-576b-a881-21f1c66c02e2', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80e44421-a5be-576b-a881-21f1c66c02e2'}, {'abstract': 'Yawning often increases the secretion of tears because of muscular tension, and it can cause a runny nose, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/12/01/science/yawning-and-the-tears-that-follow.html', 'snippet': 'Yawning often increases the secretion of tears because of muscular tension, and it can cause a runny nose, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. Why do yawns sometimes cause one’s eyes to tear?', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/12/01/science/01QNASUB/01QNASUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 358, 'width': 365, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/12/01/science/01QNASUB/01QNASUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 358, 'width': 365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/12/01/science/01QNASUB/01QNASUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/12/01/science/01QNASUB/01QNASUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/12/01/science/01QNASUB/01QNASUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Yawning and the Tears That Follow', 'kicker': 'Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yawns Squeeze the Tears Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ray, C Claiborne', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yawning', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eyes and Eyesight', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crying', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Face', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-30T17:43:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. Claiborne Ray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'Claiborne', 'lastname': 'Ray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5ac3e06-16d5-53da-a205-2e480153b9a5', 'word_count': 184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5ac3e06-16d5-53da-a205-2e480153b9a5'}, {'abstract': 'New books about sports include looks at baseball’s “endless season” and the lives of today’s greatest marathoners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/12/06/books/review/sports-the-grind-billion-dollar-ball-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books about sports include looks at baseball’s “endless season” and the lives of today’s greatest marathoners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I can’t swim. I can’t dribble a basketball without staring at it, severely compromising my mobility. And I’ve never raised my voice to a stranger, let alone my fists. In sum, I’m not an athlete. But these facts only deepen my admiration for those who are. It’s why I love sports, and the exhilarating, joyously cliché-ridden prose it inspires.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '64', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/12/06/books/review/1206-BKS-Sports/1206-BKS-Sports-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/12/06/books/review/1206-BKS-Sports/1206-BKS-Sports-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 376}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/12/06/books/review/1206-BKS-Sports/1206-BKS-Sports-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 641, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'cre