y-politicized anxiety surrounding the acts that we decide to call “terrorism.” This contrast is widely invoked as evidence of American xenophobia: We fear attacks from a nebulous or specifically-Islamic “Other” more than we fear violence by angry white men, it’s alleged, even if the Other isn’t likely to\xa0hurt us nearly as much as a bunch of heavily-armed heartlanders, because Americans still\xa0identify with\xa0whiteness/maleness/Christianity\xa0too closely to fully imagine “white Christian terrorism” as something to be feared.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We Fear When We Fear Terrorism', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boston Marathon Bombings (2013)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Crime', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Attacks (November 2015)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dear, Robert L Jr (1958- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARMY OF GOD', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Operation Rescue', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Planned Parenthood Federation of America', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City (Okla)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-11-30T19:55:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98a25d22-88c1-58be-be3a-70d42f48ac83', 'word_count': 1417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98a25d22-88c1-58be-be3a-70d42f48ac83'}, {'abstract': 'The actor on "The Big Short," playing boneheaded characters and why “The Daily Show” shifted away from snark.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/12/06/magazine/steve-carell-does-not-mock-the-undeserving.html', 'snippet': 'The actor on "The Big Short," playing boneheaded characters and why “The Daily Show” shifted away from snark.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You didn’t start out with the intention of becoming a comic actor. Now that your career has finally drifted toward dramatic acting, I’m curious: What was the vision of the kind of career that you thought you might have? I didn’t set the bar very high for myself, I’ll be honest. I didn’t have a vision. I just wanted to be employed, and I was willing to do almost everything short of pornography to continue working as an actor. I know that’s a pretty horrible image.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '90', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/12/06/magazine/06talk/06mag-06talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1107, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/12/06/magazine/06talk/06mag-06talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1107}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/12/06/magazine/06talk/06mag-06talk-t_CA0-jum